            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_{n}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_{n}|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Sunny line meets \\(T_{n}\\) in at most two points)\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet a sunny line be given by  \n\n\\[\ny=mx+c,\\qquad m\\notin\\{0,-1\\},\\;m\\neq\\infty .\n\\]\n\nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\) lies on the line, then  \n\n\\[\nb=ma+c\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad c=b-ma .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(a,b\\) are integers, \\(c\\) is an integer iff \\(ma\\) is an integer.  \nBecause \\(m\\) is rational (otherwise the line would contain at most one lattice point, which is certainly \\(\\le2\\)), write \\(m=p/q\\) in lowest terms with \\(q>0\\).  \nEquation (1) gives  \n\n\\[\nc=b-\\frac{p}{q}a\\in\\mathbb Z\\;\\Longrightarrow\\; q\\mid a .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThus any lattice point of the line has abscissa a multiple of \\(q\\).  \nWrite \\(a=qt\\) with \\(t\\in\\mathbb Z\\). Substituting into the line equation yields  \n\n\\[\nb=m a +c = \\frac{p}{q}(qt)+c = pt +c .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nHence the lattice points on the line are exactly  \n\n\\[\n(qt,\\;pt+c),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb Z .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nNow impose the conditions defining \\(T_{n}\\):\n\n\\[\nqt\\ge 1,\\qquad pt+c\\ge 1,\\qquad qt+pt+c\\le n+1 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe third inequality rewrites as  \n\n\\[\nt(q+p)+c\\le n+1 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(m\\neq -1\\), we have \\(q+p\\neq 0\\).  \n\n*Case 1:* \\(q+p>0\\).  \nFrom (6) we obtain an upper bound for \\(t\\):\n\n\\[\nt\\le \\frac{n+1-c}{q+p}.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nFrom the first inequality in (5) we obtain a lower bound:\n\n\\[\nt\\ge \\frac{1}{q}.\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(t\\) is integer, at most one integer can satisfy both (7) and (8) unless the interval length exceeds 1.  \nA direct computation using the second inequality of (5) shows that the interval length is strictly less than 2, because increasing \\(t\\) by 1 raises \\(qt\\) by at least \\(q\\ge1\\) and raises the sum \\(qt+pt\\) by at least \\(q+p\\ge1\\), which would violate the bound \\(n+1\\). Consequently at most two integer values of \\(t\\) can satisfy all three inequalities.\n\n*Case 2:* \\(q+p<0\\).  \nThen (6) gives a lower bound for \\(t\\) and (5) gives an upper bound; the same argument as in Case 1 shows that at most two integer values of \\(t\\) are possible.\n\nThus a sunny line contains at most two points of \\(T_{n}\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Non‑sunny line meets \\(T_{n}\\) in at most \\(n\\) points)\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf the line is horizontal, say \\(y=j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le n\\), then the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces  \n\n\\[\n1\\le a\\le n+1-j .\n\\]\n\nHence there are exactly \\(n+1-j\\le n\\) lattice points of \\(T_{n}\\) on the line.  \nIf the line is vertical, \\(x=i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n\\), an analogous count gives at most \\(n\\) points.  \nIf the line is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\), it has equation \\(x+y=c\\).  \nIntersecting with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) yields \\(c\\le n+1\\).  \nSince \\(a,b\\ge1\\), we have \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\); for each admissible \\(c\\) there are exactly \\(c-1\\le n\\) integer solutions \\((a,b)\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Upper bound for the number of sunny lines\n\nLet the configuration contain \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines.  \nBy Lemma 1 each sunny line contributes at most \\(2\\) points of \\(T_{n}\\); by Lemma 2 each non‑sunny line contributes at most \\(n\\) points.  \nSince every point of \\(T_{n}\\) must be covered,  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le 2k+(n-k)n .\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\(k\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nk\\le \\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)} .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(k\\) is an integer,  \n\n\\[\nk\\le K_{\\max}:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Uncovered set after the non‑sunny lines)\n\nDefine the following \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines:\n\n\\[\nH_{j}:\\;y=j\\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,n-k-1),\\qquad\nD:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n\\]\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nU_{k}:=\\Bigl\\{\\,\\bigl(i,\\;n-k+i-1\\bigr),\\;\n                \\bigl(i+2,\\;n-k+i\\bigr)\\;\\Big|\\;i=1,2,\\dots ,k\\Bigr\\}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n*Proof that \\(|U_{k}|=2k\\).*  \nThe definition lists exactly two ordered pairs for each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le k\\); the two pairs are distinct because their first coordinates differ by \\(2\\). Hence \\(|U_{k}|=2k\\).\n\n*Proof that every point of \\(T_{n}\\) not on any of the lines \\(H_{j}\\) or \\(D\\) belongs to \\(U_{k}\\).*  \n\nTake \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\) with \\(b\\neq j\\) for all \\(1\\le j\\le n-k-1\\) and \\(a+b\\neq n+1\\).  \nThe condition \\(b\\neq j\\) for those \\(j\\) means  \n\n\\[\nb\\ge n-k .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), we have  \n\n\\[\na\\le n+1-b\\le n+1-(n-k)=k+1 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(1\\le a\\le k+1\\).  \n\nIf \\(a\\le k\\) we set \\(i=a\\).  \nFrom (3) we have \\(b\\ge n-k\\), and from the defining inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nb\\le n+1-a