The third inequality simplifies to  \n\n\\[\n3t+ n-k+i+1 \\le n+1\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;3t\\le k-i .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(0\\le i\\le k\\), the right‑hand side of (9) is a non‑negative integer less than or equal to \\(k-1\\).  \nThus \\(t\\) can only be \\(0\\) or, possibly, \\(1\\) when \\(k-i\\ge3\\).  \n\nWe now examine the two candidates.\n\n*If \\(t=0\\):*  \nFrom (7) we obtain the point \\((1,\\;n-k+i)\\).  \nBecause \\(i\\ge1\\) and \\(k\\le K_{\\max}<n\\), we have \\(1\\le 1\\le n\\) and \\(n-k+i\\ge n-k+1\\ge 1\\).  \nMoreover  \n\n\\[\n1+(n-k+i)=n-k+i+1\\le n+1,\n\\]\n\nso the point lies in \\(T_{n}\\).  \nWhen \\(i=1\\) this point is \\((1,n-k+1)\\); when \\(i>1\\) it coincides with the point \\(P_{i}=(i,n-k+i-1)\\) after shifting the index: indeed, substituting \\(t=0\\) into (7) with the original parametrisation \\(x=2t+1\\) gives \\(x=1\\); to obtain the general \\(P_{i}\\) we note that the line \\(S_{i}\\) also passes through \\((i,n-k+i-1)\\) because inserting \\(x=i\\) into the line equation yields  \n\n\\[\ny=\\frac12 i+n-k+i-\\frac12 = n-k+i-1 .\n\\]\n\nThus \\((i,n-k+i-1)\\in S_{i}\\cap T_{n}\\).\n\n*If \\(t=1\\):*  \nThen (7) gives the point \\((3,\\;n-k+i+1)\\).  \nThe sum of coordinates is  \n\n\\[\n3+(n-k+i+1)=n-k+i+4 .\n\\]\n\nFor this to be \\(\\le n+1\\) we need \\(i\\le k-3\\).  \nWhen this inequality holds, the point coincides with  \n\n\\[\nQ_{i}=(i+2,\\;n-k+i),\n\\]\n\nbecause substituting \\(x=i+2\\) into the line equation yields  \n\n\\[\ny=\\frac12(i+2)+n-k+i-\\frac12 = n-k+i .\n\\]\n\nHence the only lattice points of \\(S_{i}\\) that satisfy the constraints (8) are precisely  \n\n\\[\nP_{i}=(i,\\;n-k+i-1),\\qquad Q_{i}=(i+2,\\;n-k+i),\n\\]\n\nboth of which belong to the set \\(U_{k}\\) defined in (2).  \n\nNo other integer \\(t\\) satisfies (9), so there are no further points of \\(T_{n}\\) on \\(S_{i}\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Completion of the construction\n\nFor a