g the previously identified gaps.\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_{n}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_{n}|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Sunny line meets \\(T_{n}\\) in at most two points)\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet a sunny line be given by  \n\n\\[\ny=mx+c,\\qquad m\\notin\\{0,-1\\},\\;m\\neq\\infty .\n\\]\n\nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\) lies on the line, then  \n\n\\[\nb=ma+c\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad c=b-ma .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(a,b\\) are integers, \\(c\\) is an integer iff \\(ma\\) is an integer.  \nBecause \\(m\\) is rational (otherwise the line would contain at most one lattice point, which is certainly \\(\\le2\\)), write \\(m=p/q\\) in lowest terms with \\(q>0\\).  \nEquation (1) gives  \n\n\\[\nc=b-\\frac{p}{q}a\\in\\mathbb Z\\;\\Longrightarrow\\; q\\mid a .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThus any lattice point of the line has abscissa a multiple of \\(q\\).  \nWrite \\(a=qt\\) with \\(t\\in\\mathbb Z\\). Substituting into the line equation yields  \n\n\\[\nb=m a +c = \\frac{p}{q}(qt)+c = pt +c .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nHence the lattice points on the line are exactly  \n\n\\[\n(qt,\\;pt+c),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb Z .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nNow impose the conditions defining \\(T_{n}\\):\n\n\\[\nqt\\ge 1,\\qquad pt+c\\ge 1,\\qquad qt+pt+c\\le n+1 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe third inequality rewrites as  \n\n\\[\nt(q+p)+c\\le n+1 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(m\\neq -1\\), we have \\(q+p\\neq 0\\).  \n\n*Case 1:* \\(q+p>0\\).  \nFrom (6) we obtain an upper bound for \\(t\\):\n\n\\[\nt\\le \\frac{n+1-c}{q+p}.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nFrom the first inequality in (5) we obtain a lower bound:\n\n\\[\nt\\ge \\frac{1}{q}.\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(t\\) is integer, at most one integer can satisfy both (7) and (8) u