on.  \n\nThus every uncovered point is one of the \\(2k\\) points listed in \\(U_{k}\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4 (Sunny lines cover exactly the two intended points)\n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_{i}:\\; y=\\frac12 x+\\Bigl(n-k+i-\\frac12\\Bigr).\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(\\frac12\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence each \\(S_{i}\\) is sunny.\n\n*Parametrisation of lattice points on \\(S_{i}\\).*  \nWrite the equation as  \n\n\\[\n2y = x + 2\\bigl(n-k+i-\\tfrac12\\bigr)=x+2n-2k+2i-1 .\n\\]\n\nThus a lattice point \\((x,y)\\) on \\(S_{i}\\) must satisfy  \n\n\\[\nx\\equiv 1\\pmod 2 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nLet \\(x=2t+1\\) with \\(t\\in\\mathbb Z\\). Substituting into the line equation gives  \n\n\\[\ny = \\frac12(2t+1)+n-k+i-\\frac12 = t+n-k+i .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nConsequently every lattice point of \\(S_{i}\\) has the form  \n\n\\[\n(2t+1,\\;t+n-k+i),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb Z .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n*Intersection with \\(T_{n}\\).*  \nWe require  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\n2t+1\\ge 1,\\\\[2pt]\nt+n-k+i\\ge 1,\\\\[2pt]\n(2t+1)+(t+n-k+i)\\le n+1 .\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThe first inequality gives \\(t\\ge 0\\).  \nThe second inequality yields \\(t\\ge k-i+1-n\\), which is automatically satisfied for \\(t\\ge0\\) because \\(i\\le k\\) and \\(n\\ge3\\).  \n\nThe third inequality simplifies to  \n\n\\[\n3t+ n-k+i+1 \\le n+1\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;3t\\le k-i .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(0\\le i\\le k\\), the right‑hand side of (9) is a non‑negative integer less than or equal to \\(k-1\\).  \nThus \\(t\\) can only be \\(0\\) or, possibly, \\(1\\) when \\(k-i\\ge3\\).  \n\nWe now examine the two candidates.\n\n*If \\(t=0\\):*  \nFrom (7) we obtain the point \\((1,\\;n-k+i)\\).  \nBecause \\(i\\ge1\\) and \\(k\\le K_{\\max}<n\\), we have \\(1\\le 1\\le n\\) and \\(n-k+i\\ge n-k+1\\ge 1\\).  \nMoreover  \n\n\\[\n1+(n-k+i)=n-k+i+1\\le n+1,\n\\]\n\nso the point lies in \\(T