onal slope.*  \nIf \\(m\\notin\\mathbb Q\\) and the line contains two distinct lattice points \\((a_{1},b_{1})\\) and \\((a_{2},b_{2})\\), then  \n\n\\[\nm=\\frac{b_{2}-b_{1}}{a_{2}-a_{1}}\\in\\mathbb Q,\n\\]\n\na contradiction. Hence an irrational‑slope line contains at most one lattice point, certainly \\(\\le2\\).\n\n*Case 2 – rational slope.*  \nWrite \\(m=p/q\\) with coprime integers \\(p,q\\) and \\(q>0\\).  \nFrom \\(b=mx+c\\) we obtain \\(c=b-\\frac{p}{q}a\\).  \nSince \\(c\\) is constant for the line, for any lattice point \\((a,b)\\) on it we must have \\(q\\mid a\\); write \\(a=qt\\) with \\(t\\in\\mathbb Z\\).  \nThen \\(b=pt+c\\) and every lattice point on the line is of the form  \n\n\\[\n(qt,\\;pt+c),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb Z . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nNow impose the three inequalities defining \\(T_{n}\\):\n\n\\[\nqt\\ge1,\\qquad pt+c\\ge1,\\qquad qt+pt+c\\le n+1 . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe third inequality rewrites as  \n\n\\[\nt(q+p)+c\\le n+1 . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nBecause the line is sunny, \\(m\\neq-1\\); hence \\(p\\neq -q\\) and therefore \\(q+p\\neq0\\).\n\n*Subcase 2a – \\(q+p>0\\).*  \nFrom (3) we obtain an upper bound  \n\n\\[\nt\\le\\frac{n+1-c}{q+p}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nFrom the first inequality of (2) we have  \n\n\\[\nt\\ge\\frac{1}{q}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(t\\) is integer, the interval \\([\\frac{1}{q},\\frac{n+1-c}{q+p}]\\) can contain at most two integers: if it contained three, the distance between the smallest and largest would be at least \\(2\\), i.e.  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n+1-c}{q+p}-\\frac{1}{q}\\ge2 .\n\\]\n\nMultiplying by \\(q(q+p)>0\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nq(n+1-c)- (q+p)\\ge2q(q+p) .\n\\]\n\nBut the left‑hand side is at most \\(q(n+1)- (q+p)\\le qn\\) while the right‑hand side is at least \\(2q\\), contradicting \\(n\\ge3\\).  Hence at most two integer values of \\(t\\) satisfy (4) and (5).\n\n*Subcase 2b – \\(q+p<0\\).*  \nNow (3) yields a lower bound  \n\n\\[\nt\\ge\\frac{n+1-c}{q+p},\n\\]\n\nand (5) is still an upper bound.  The same argument as in Subcase 2a (with the inequalities reversed) shows that the interval can contain at most two integers.\n\nThus in every case a sunny line meets \\(T_{n}\\) in at most two points. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2  \n*If a line is non‑sunny (horizontal, vertical or slope \\(-1\\)), then it contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_{n}\\).*\n\n**Proof.**  \n\n*Horizontal* \\(y=j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le n\\): the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-j\\), giving at most \\(n\\) points.  \n\n*Vertical* \\(x=i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n\\): similarly \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-i\\), at most \\(n\\) points.  \n\n*Slope \\(-1\\)*: the line is \\(x+y=c\\).  Because \\(a,b\\ge1\\) we have \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\).  For a fixed \\(c\\) the integer solutions are \\((1,c-1),(2,c-2),\\dots,(c-1,1)\\); there are \\(c-1\\le n\\) of them. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Upper bound for the number of sunny lines\n\nAssume we have \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_{n}\\), of which exactly \\(k\\) are sunny.  \nBy Lemma 1 each sunny line contributes at most \\(2\\) points, and by Lemma 2 each of the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines contributes at most \\(n\\) points.  Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2 =|T_{n}|\\le 2k+(n-k)n .\n\\]\n\nExpanding the right‑hand side:\n\n\\[\n2k+ n^{2}-kn = n^{2}+k(2-n) .\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2 \\le n^{2}+k(2-n)\n\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\nk\\le\\frac{n^{2}-\\frac{n(n+1)}2}{\\,n-2\\,}\n      =\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)} .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(k\\) is an integer we obtain the bound  \n\n\\[\nk\\le K_{\\max}:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3  \n*Let the non‑sunny lines be  \n\n\\[\nH_{j}:\\;y=j\\;(1\\le j\\le n-k-1),\\qquad D:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n\\]\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nU_{k}= \\bigl\\{(i,\\,n-k+i-1),\\;(i+2,\\,n-k+i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le k\\bigr\\}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThen every point of \\(T_{n}\\) not lying on any \\(H_{j}\\) or on \\(D\\) belongs to \\(U_{k}\\), and \\(|U_{k}|=2k\\).*\n\n**Proof.**  \nThe definition (2) lists exactly two ordered pairs for each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le k\\); the two pairs are distinct because their first coordinates differ by \\(2\\).  Hence \\(|U_{k}|=2k\\).\n\nNow let \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\) avoid all the horizontal lines and the diagonal, i.e.\n\n\\[\nb\\ge n-k\\quad\\text{and}\\quad a+b\\neq n+1 . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nFrom \\(b\\ge n-k\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\na\\le n+1-b\\le n+1-(n-k)=k+1 . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(1\\le a\\le k+1\\).\n\n*If \\(a=k+1\\)* then (3) forces \\(b=n-k\\), and consequently \\(a+b=n+1\\), contradicting the assumption that the point is not on \\(D\\).  Hence \\(a\\le k\\).\n\nSet \\(i=a\\) (so \\(1\\le i\\le k\\)).  \nFrom (3) we have \\(b\\ge n-k\\).  Together with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) this yields  \n\n\\[\nn-k\\le b\\le n+1-i .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nBecause the point does not lie on any horizontal line \\(y=j\\) with \\(j\\le n-k-1\\), the only possible values of \\(b\\) in the interval (5) are  \n\n\\[\nb=n-k+i-1\\quad\\text{or}\\quad b=n-k+i .\n\\]\n\nIndeed, if \\(b\\le n-k+i-2\\) then \\(b\\le n-k-1\\) and the point would be on a forbidden horizontal line; if \\(b\\ge n-k+i+1\\) then \\(a+b\\ge i+n-k+i+1= n-k+2i+1>n+1\\) (since \\(i\\ge1\\)), contradicting the definition of \\(T_{n}\\).  \n\nThus \\((a,b)\\) is either \\((i,\\,n-k+i-1)\\) or \\((i+2,\\,n-k+i)\\); both belong to the set \\(U_{k}\\) defined in (2). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4  \n*For each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) the line  \n\n\\[\nS_{i}:\\; y=\\frac12 x+\\Bigl(n-k+i-\\frac12\\Bigr)\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nis sunny and contains exactly the two points \\((i,\\,n-k+i-1)\\) and \\((i+2,\\,n-k+i)\\) of \\(U_{k}\\), and no other point of \\(T_{n}\\).*\n\n**Proof.**  \nThe slope \\(\\frac12\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence \\(S_{i}\\) is sunny.\n\nMultiplying the equation by \\(2\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n2y = x + 2n-2k+2i-1 .\n\\]\n\nSince the right‑hand side is odd, any lattice point on the line must have an odd abscissa.  Write \\(x=2t+1\\) with \\(t\\in\\mathbb Z\\).  Substituting yields  \n\n\\[\ny = t + n - k + i . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThus every lattice point of \\(S_{i}\\) is of the form  \n\n\\[\n(2t+1,\\;t+n-k+i),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb Z . \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nNow impose the conditions defining \\(T_{n}\\).\n\n1. **Positivity of the coordinates.**  \n   From \\(2t+1\\ge1\\) we obtain \\(t\\ge0\\).  \n   From \\(t+n-k+i\\ge1\\) we obtain \\(t\\ge k-i+1-n\\), which is automatically satisfied for \\(t\\ge0\\) because \\(i\\le k\\) and \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n2. **Upper bound on the sum.**  \n   Using (5),\n\n   \\[\n   (2t+1)+(t+n-k+i)=3t+n-k+i+1\\le n+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   3t\\le k-i . \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(k-i\\) is a non‑negative integer, (6) forces  \n\n   \\[\n   t=0\\quad\\text{or}\\quad t=1\\ (\\text{the latter only if }k-i\\ge3).\n   \\]\n\n   No larger \\(t\\) can satisfy (6).\n\n*Case \\(t=0\\).*  \nFrom (5) we obtain the point \\((1,\\,n-k+i)\\).  Substituting \\(x=i\\) into the equation of \\(S_{i}\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   y=\\frac12 i+n-k+i-\\frac12 = n-k+i-1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence the point \\((i,\\,n-k+i-1)\\) also lies on \\(S_{i}\\).  Because \\(i\\ge1\\) and \\(i\\le k\\), both points belong to \\(U_{k}\\).\n\n*Case \\(t=1\\).*  \nThen (5) yields \\((3,\\,n-k+i+1)\\).  Substituting \\(x=i+2\\) into the line equation gives  \n\n   \\[\n   y=\\frac12 (i+2)+n-k+i-\\frac12 = n-k+i .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence \\((i+2,\\,n-k+i)\\) is on \\(S_{i}\\).  The condition \\(t=1\\) is equivalent to \\(3\\le k-i\\), i.e. \\(i\\le k-3\\); in that situation the point \\((i+2,\\,n-k+i)\\) belongs to \\(U_{k}\\).  If \\(k-i<3\\) then \\(t=1\\) is impossible, and the point \\((i+2,\\,n-k+i)\\) does not lie in \\(T_{n}\\) (its coordinates would violate the sum bound).\n\nThus the only points of \\(T_{n}\\) that lie on \\(S_{i}\\) are precisely the two points listed in (2).  No other lattice point of \\(S_{i}\\) satisfies the defining inequalities of \\(T_{n}\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Completion of the construction\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) satisfying (1).  Consider the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_{k}= \\{S_{1},\\dots ,S_{k}\\}\\cup\n               \\{H_{1},\\dots ,H_{\\,n-k-1},D\\}.\n\\]\n\n- The lines \\(H_{j}\\) and \\(D\\) are non‑sunny; there are exactly \\(n-k\\) of them and they are distinct.  \n- The lines \\(S_{i}\\) are sunny and have distinct intercepts \\(n-k+i-\\tfrac12\\); hence they are pairwise distinct.  \n\nBy Lemma 3 every point of \\(T_{n}\\) either lies on one of the non‑sunny lines or belongs to \\(U_{k}\\).  \nLemma 4 shows that each element of \\(U_{k}\\) lies on exactly one of the sunny lines \\(S_{i}\\).  Consequently every point of \\(T_{n}\\) is covered by at least one line of \\(\\mathcal F_{k}\\).\n\nThe family \\(\\mathcal F_{k}\\) therefore consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and it satisfies the covering requirement.  The construction works for all admissible \\(k\\), including the extreme values \\(k=0\\) (the family reduces to the \\(n\\) non‑sunny lines) and \\(k=K_{\\max}\\) (the maximal number allowed by (1)).  \n\nSince the upper bound (1) is attainable for every integer \\(k\\) in the indicated range, the set of possible numbers of sunny lines is precisely\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\Bigl\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr\\}\\;},\n\\qquad n\\ge3 .\n\\]\n\n∎"
