-k+i-1)\\) and \\((i+2,\\,n-k+i)\\) of \\(U_{k}\\), and no other point of \\(T_{n}\\).*\n\n**Proof.**  \nThe slope \\(\\frac12\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence \\(S_{i}\\) is sunny.\n\nMultiplying the equation by \\(2\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n2y = x + 2n-2k+2i-1 .\n\\]\n\nSince the right\u2011hand side is odd, any lattice point on the line must have an odd abscissa.  Write \\(x=2t+1\\) with \\(t\\in\\mathbb Z\\).  Substituting yields  \n\n\\[\ny = t + n - k + i . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThus every lattice point of \\(S_{i}\\) is of the form  \n\n\\[\n(2t+1,\\;t+n-k+i),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb Z . \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nNow impose the conditions defining \\(T_{n}\\).\n\n1. **Positivity of the coordinates.**  \n   From \\(2t+1\\ge1\\) we obtain \\(t\\ge0\\).  \n   From \\(t+n-k+i\\ge1\\) we obtain \\(t\\ge k-i+1-n\\), which is automatically satisfied for \\(t\\ge0\\) because \\(i\\le k\\) and \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n2. **Upper bound on the sum.**  \n   Using (5),\n\n   \\[\n   (2t+1)+(t+n-k+i)=3t+n-k+i+1\\le n+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   3t\\le k-i . \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(k-i\\) is a non\u2011negative integer, (6) forces  \n\n   \\[\n   t=0\\quad\\text{or}\\quad t=1\\ (\\text{the latter only if }k-i\\ge3).\n   \\]\n\n   No larger \\(t\\) can satisfy (6).\n\n*Case \\(t=0\\).*  \nFrom (5) we obtain the point \\((1,\\,n-k+i)\\).  Substituting \\(x=i\\) into the equation of \\(S_{i}\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   y=\\frac12 i+n-k+i-\\frac12 = n-k+i-1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence the point \\((i,\\,n-k+i-1)\\) also lies on \\(S_{i}\\).  Because \\(i\\ge1\\) and \\(i\\le k\\), both points belong to \\(U_{k}\\).\n\n*Case \\(t=1\\).*  \nThen (5) yields \\((3,\\,n-k+i+1)\\).  Substituting \\(x=i+2\\) into the line equation gives  \n\n   \\[\n   y=\\frac12 (i+2)+n-k+i-\\frac12 = n-k+i .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence \\((i+2,\\,n-k+i)\\) is on \\(S_{i}\\).  The condition \\(t=1\\) is equivalent to \\(3\\le k-i\\), i.e. \\(i\\le k-3\\); in that situation the point \\((i+2,\\,n-k+i)\\) belongs to