(H_{j}\\)) and \\(a+b\\neq n+1\\).  From \\(b\\ge n-k\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we get \\(a\\le k+1\\).  If \\(a=k+1\\) then \\(b=n-k\\) and \\(a+b=n+1\\), contradicting the assumption, so \\(1\\le a\\le k\\).  Put \\(i=a\\).\n\nBecause \\(b\\ge n-k\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have  \n\n\\[\nn-k\\le b\\le n+1-i .\n\\]\n\nIf \\(b=n-k+i-1\\) we obtain the point \\((i,\\,n-k+i-1)\\).  \nIf \\(b=n-k+i\\) we obtain the point \\((i,\\,n-k+i)\\); however this point does **not** satisfy \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) when \\(i\\ge k-1\\).  In those cases the only admissible value is \\(b=n-k+i-1\\), and the second point of (2) is instead realised by taking \\(a=i+2\\) (which is still \\(\\le k\\) for \\(i\\le k-3\\)).  A straightforward verification shows that the two points listed in (2) are exactly the possibilities for \\((a,b)\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4  \n*For each \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_{i}:\\; y=\\frac12x+\\Bigl(n-k+i-\\frac12\\Bigr).\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThen \\(S_{i}\\) is sunny and contains the point \\((i,\\,n-k+i-1)\\).  \nIf \\(i\\le k-3\\) it also contains the point \\((i+2,\\,n-k+i)\\); for \\(i=k,\\,k-1,\\,k-2\\) the second point lies outside \\(T_{n}\\).*\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe slope \\(\\frac12\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), so the line is sunny.  \nBoth points satisfy the equation of \\(S_{i}\\) because their difference is \\((2,1)\\), i.e. slope \\(\\frac12\\).  The first point always belongs to \\(T_{n}\\) (its coordinates are positive and their sum is \\(n\\)).  \n\nFor the second point we compute the sum  \n\n\\[\n(i+2)+(n-k+i)=n+2i-k+2 .\n\\]\n\nThe condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) is equivalent to \\(2i\\le k-1\\).  This holds precisely when \\(i\\le k-3\\).  Hence for \\(i\\le k-3\\) the second point is also in \\(T_{n}\\); for the three largest indices it is not. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Completion of the construction\n\nFi