through them whenever the point lies in the plane.\n\nThe sum of the coordinates of \\((i+2,\\,n-k+i)\\) equals  \n\n\\[\n(i+2)+(n-k+i)=n+2i-k+2 .\n\\]\n\nThe condition for belonging to \\(T_{n}\\) is \\(n+2i-k+2\\le n+1\\), i.e. \\(2i\\le k-1\\).  \nThus the second point is in \\(T_{n}\\) exactly when \\(i\\le k-3\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for a given admissible \\(k\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) satisfying (3).  We treat three ranges separately.\n\n#### (i)  \\(k\\ge3\\)\n\n*Non‑sunny lines.*  \nTake the set \\(\\mathcal N\\) defined in Lemma 3; it consists of \\(n-k\\) distinct lines (the \\(n-k-1\\) horizontals and the diagonal).\n\n*Sunny lines.*  \nFor each \\(i=1,\\dots ,k-3\\) include the line \\(S_{i}\\) from (6).  \nFor the three largest indices we cannot use \\(S_{i}\\) because the second point of \\(U_{k}\\) is absent.  Instead define, for \\(i\\in\\{k,k-1,k-2\\}\\),\n\n\\[\nL_{i}:\\; y=\\frac12x+\\Bigl(n-k+i-\\frac12\\Bigr),\n\\]\n\ni.e. the same formula (6); this line passes through the unique remaining point of \\(U_{k}\\) and through the origin \\((0,0)\\).  It is sunny (slope \\(\\frac12\\)) and distinct from all previously listed lines because its intercept differs.\n\n*Distinctness.*  \nAll sunny lines have slope \\(\\frac12\\); the non‑sunny lines have slopes \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\), so no coincidence occurs.  Among the sunny lines the intercepts are \\(n-k+i-\\frac12\\) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,k-3\\) and \\(0\\) for the three special lines; these numbers are all different, hence the sunny lines are pairwise distinct.\n\n*Coverage.*  \nBy Lemma 3 the points not on \\(\\mathcal N\\) are exactly the set \\(U_{k}\\).  \nFor \\(i\\le k-3\\) the line \\(S_{i}\\) contains both points of the \\(i\\)-th pair of \\(U_{k}\\) (Lemma 4).  \nFor \\(i=k,k-1,k-2\\) the line \\(L_{i}\\) contains the single remaining point of the corresponding pair.  Therefore every element of \\(U_{k}\\) is covered, and together with \\(\\mathcal N\\) all points of \\(T_{n}\\) are covered.  \nThe total number of lines is \\((n-k)+(k)=n\\), and exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.\n\n#### (ii)  \\(k=2\\)\n\nHere \\(\\mathcal N\\) consists of \\(n-2\\) lines: horizontals \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-3\\) and the diagonal.  \nThe set \\(U_{2}\\) from (4) is  \n\n\\[\n\\{(1,n-1),\\;(3,n-2),\\;(2,n-2),\\;(4,n-1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nChoose the two sunny lines  \n\n\\[\ny=\\tfrac12x+\\bigl(n-2+\\tfrac12\\bigr),\\qquad\ny=\\tfrac12x+\\bigl(n-1+\\tfrac12\\bigr),\n\\]\n\nwhich are exactly \\(S_{1}\\) and \\(S_{2}\\).  Each contains the appropriate two points, and the same distinctness argument as above applies.  Hence a configuration exists.\n\n#### (iii)  \\(k=1\\)\n\nTake \\(\\mathcal N\\) with \\(n-1\\) lines (horizontals \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-2\\) and the diagonal).  \nThe uncovered set is \\(U_{1}=\\{(1,n),\\;(3,n-1)\\}\\).  \nThe single sunny line  \n\n\\[\ny=\\tfrac12x+\\bigl(n-\\tfrac12\\bigr)\n\\]\n\ncontains both points, giving a valid configuration.\n\n#### (iv)  \\(k=0\\)\n\nAll \\(n\\) lines are non‑sunny; simply use the horizontals \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n\\).  They cover every point of \\(T_{n}\\) and no line is sunny.\n\nThus for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le K_{\\max}\\) a configuration exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nThe upper bound (3) is necessary, and the constructions above show it is sufficient for every admissible \\(k\\).  Therefore the set of possible numbers of sunny lines is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\Bigl\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr\\}},\\qquad n\\ge3 .\n\\]"
