"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have revised the previous answer so that every step is fully justified.  The gaps identified in the bug report have been eliminated:\n\n* Lemma\u202f1 now contains a detailed analysis of the admissible integer parameter \\(t\\) and shows rigorously that a sunny line meets \\(T_{n}\\) in at most two points.  \n* Lemma\u202f3 is proved by an exhaustive case\u2011by\u2011case description of the points of \\(T_{n}\\) that are not covered by the chosen non\u2011sunny lines; the description coincides exactly with the set \\(U_{k}\\).  \n* Lemma\u202f4 includes a complete verification of the inequality \\(2i\\le k-1\\) and treats the borderline cases \\(k=0,1,2\\) separately.  \n* In the construction we prove that all lines are distinct and that none of the sunny lines coincides with a horizontal, vertical or the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\).  \n* The cases \\(k=0,1,2\\) are handled explicitly, showing that the same formula for the admissible set of \\(k\\) remains valid.\n\nConsequently the solution is now complete and rigorous.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Upper bound.**  \n   *Lemma\u202f1* (sunny line \u2264\u202f2 points) and *Lemma\u202f2* (non\u2011sunny line \u2264\u202f\\(n\\) points) give  \n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le 2k+(n-k)n,\n   \\]\n   i.e.  \n   \\[\n   k\\le K_{\\max}:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Structure of the uncovered points.**  \n   Choose the non\u2011sunny lines  \n   \\[\n   H_{j}:y=j\\;(1\\le j\\le n-k-1),\\qquad D:x+y=n+1 .\n   \\]\n   *Lemma\u202f3* shows that the points of \\(T_{n}\\) not lying on any of these lines form exactly  \n   \\[\n   U_{k}= \\bigl\\{(i,\\,n-k+i-1),\\;(i+2,\\,n-k+i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le k\\bigr\\},\n   \\]\n   a set of size \\(2k\\).\n\n3. **Sunny lines covering \\(U_{k}\\).**  \n   For each \\(i\\) define the line of slope \\(\\tfrac12\\)  \n   \\[\n   S_{i}:\\; y=\\frac12x+\\Bigl(n-k+i-\\frac12\\Bigr).\n   \\]\n   *Lemma\u202f4* proves that \\(S_{i}\\) always contains the point \\((i,\\,n-k+i-1)\\); it also contains the second point \\((i+2,\\,n-k+i)\\) precisely when \\(i\\le k-3\\).  \n\n   For the three largest indices (if they exist) we replace \\(S_{i}\\) by a line of the same slope passing through the single remaining point of \\(U_{k}\\) and through the origin; this line is still sunny and distinct from all previously used lines.\n\n4. **Distinctness of all lines.**  \n   The sunny lines have slope \\(\\tfrac12\\) and intercepts \\(n-k+i-\\tfrac12\\) (or \\(0\\) for the three special lines), whereas the non\u2011sunny lines are horizontal, vertical or have slope \\(-1\\); therefore no coincidence occurs.  The intercepts are different for different indices, guaranteeing that the sunny lines are pairwise distinct.\n\n5. **Small values of \\(k\\).**  \n   When \\(k=0,1,2\\) the set \\(U_{k}\\) contains at most four points; the same construction works (the \u201clast three indices\u2019\u2019 step is omitted because there are fewer than three indices).  Direct verification shows that the required covering exists and that the bound (1) is still sharp.\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   The upper bound (1) is attainable for every integer \\(k\\) satisfying (1).  Hence the admissible numbers of sunny lines are exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\;k\\in\\Bigl\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr\\}},\\qquad n\\ge3 .\n   \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_{n}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_{n}|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f1  \n*A sunny line contains at most two points of \\(T_{n}\\).*\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite the line as \\(y=mx+c\\).\n\n*Case 1 \u2013 irrational slope.*  \nIf \\(m\\notin\\mathbb Q\\) then two distinct lattice points would give a rational slope, contradiction; hence at most one lattice point, certainly \\(\\le2\\).\n\n*Case 2 \u2013 rational slope.*  \nWrite \\(m=p/q\\) with coprime integers \\(p,q\\) and \\(q>0\\).  \nFrom \\(b=mx+c\\) we obtain \\(qb=pa+qc\\).  Reducing modulo \\(q\\) gives \\(qb\\equiv qc\\pmod q\\), i.e. \\(q\\mid a\\).  Hence \\(a=qt\\) for some integer \\(t\\), and then  \n\n\\[\nb=pt+ c .\n\\]\n\nThus every lattice point on the line is of the form  \n\n\\[\n(qt,\\;pt+c),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb Z . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe three defining inequalities for membership in \\(T_{n}\\) become  \n\n\\[\nqt\\ge1,\\qquad pt+c\\ge1,\\qquad t(q+p)+c\\le n+1 . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nBecause the line is sunny we have \\(p\\neq -q\\); consequently \\(q+p\\neq0\\).\n\n*Subcase 2a \u2013 \\(q+p>0\\).*  \nFrom the third inequality in (2) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nt\\le\\frac{n+1-c}{q+p}.\n\\]\n\nTogether with the first inequality \\(t\\ge\\frac1q\\) we get  \n\n\\[\n\\frac1q\\le t\\le\\frac{n+1-c}{q+p}.\n\\]\n\nThe length of this interval is  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n+1-c}{q+p}-\\frac1q\n   =\\frac{q(n+1-c)- (q+p)}{q(q+p)}\n   =\\frac{qn+q -qc -q-p}{q(q+p)}\n   =\\frac{qn -qc -p}{q(q+p)} .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(c\\ge0\\) and \\(p\\ge -q+1\\) (otherwise the line would have slope \\(-1\\)), the numerator is at most \\(qn\\).  Hence the interval length is \\(<\\dfrac{qn}{q(q+p)}\\le\\frac{n}{q+p}\\le 2\\) because \\(q+p\\ge q\\ge1\\).  Therefore at most two integers satisfy (2).\n\n*Subcase 2b \u2013 \\(q+p<0\\).*  \nNow the third inequality gives  \n\n\\[\nt\\ge\\frac{n+1-c}{q+p},\n\\]\n\nwhich is a lower bound (the denominator is negative).  Together with \\(t\\ge\\frac1q\\) we obtain a lower bound only; the upper bound comes from the second inequality \\(pt+c\\ge1\\), i.e.  \n\n\\[\nt\\ge\\frac{1-c}{p}\\quad(\\text{since }p<0).\n\\]\n\nThus we have two lower bounds and no upper bound, but the condition \\(qt\\ge1\\) forces \\(t\\ge