er bound for the number of sunny lines\n\nAssume we have \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_{n}\\), exactly \\(k\\) of them sunny.  \nBy Lemma 1 each sunny line contributes at most \\(2\\) points, and by Lemma 2 each of the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines contributes at most \\(n\\) points.  Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le 2k+(n-k)n .\n\\]\n\nRearranging gives  \n\n\\[\nk\\le\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)} .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(k\\) is integral we obtain the necessary condition  \n\n\\[\nk\\le K_{\\max}:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3  \n*Let  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal N=\\{H_{j}:y=j\\mid 1\\le j\\le n-k-1\\}\\cup\\{D:x+y=n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nU_{k}= \\bigl\\{(i,\\,n-k+i-1),\\;(i+2,\\,n-k+i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le k\\bigr\\}.\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nThen every point of \\(T_{n}\\) that is not on any line of \\(\\mathcal N\\) belongs to \\(U_{k}\\), and \\(|U_{k}|=2k\\).*\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe two families in (10) are disjoint, so \\(|U_{k}|=2k\\).\n\nTake \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\) with \\(b\\notin\\{1,\\dots ,n-k-1\\}\\) (otherwise it lies on some \\(H_{j}\\)) and \\(a+b\\neq n+1\\) (otherwise it lies on \\(D\\)).  \nThus \\(b\\ge n-k\\).  From \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\na\\le n+1-b\\le n+1-(n-k)=k+1 .\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(a=k+1\\) then \\(b\\ge n-k\\) forces \\(a+b\\ge n+1\\); the equality case would place the point on \\(D\\), which we have excluded.  Hence \\(a\\le k\\).  Put \\(i=a\\) (so \\(1\\le i\\le k\\)).\n\nNow \\(b\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\nn-k\\le b\\le n+1-i . \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(b\\) is an integer, the only possibilities compatible with (12) are  \n\n\\[\nb=n-k+i-1\\quad\\text{or}\\quad b=n-k+i .\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\n*If \\(b=n-k+i-1\\)*, then \\((a,b)=(i,\\,n-k+i-1)\\), which is the first type of point in (10).\n\n*If \\(b=n-k+i\\)*, then (12) gives \\(i\\le k-1\\).  For such \\(i\\) the point \\((i,\\,n-k+i)\\) does **not** belong to \\(T_{n}\\) because its sum equals \\(n+2i-k+1>n+1\\) when \\(i\\ge\\frac{k+1}{2}\\).  The only way to satisfy (12) with \\(b=n-k+i\\) is to increase the first coordinate by \\(2\\): the point \\((i+2,\\,n-k+i)\\) has sum  \n\n\\[\n(i+2)+(n-k+i)=n+2i-k+2\\le n+1\n\\]\n\nexactly when \\(2i\\le k-1\\), i.e. \\(i\\le k-3\\).  In that case \\((i+2,\\,n-k+i)\\) is precisely the second type of point in (10).  When \\(i=k-2,k-1,k\\) the second point lies outside \\(T_{n}\\), so no further points arise.\n\nThus every point of \\(T_{n}\\) not covered by \\(\\mathcal N\\) belongs to the set described in (10), i.e. to \\(U_{k}\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4  \n*For each \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_{i}:\\; y=\\frac12x+\\Bigl(n-k+i-\\frac12\\Bigr).\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nThen \\(S_{i}\\) is sunny and contains the point \\((i,\\,n-k+i-1)\\).  \nMoreover \\(S_{i}\\) also contains \\((i+2,\\,n-k+i)\\) **iff** \\(i\\le k-3\\).*\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe slope \\(\\frac12\\) differs from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence \\(S_{i}\\) is sunny.  \nBoth points \\((i,\\,n-k+i-1)\\) and \\((i+2,\\,n-k+i)\\) differ by the vector \\((2,1)\\), whose slope is \\(\\frac12\\); substituting either point into (14) verifies that they satisfy the equation, so the line passes through them whenever the point lies in the plane.\n\nThe point \\((i+2,\\,n-k+i)\\) belongs to \\(T_{n}\\) exactly when  \n\n\\[\n(i+2)+(n-k+i)=n+2i-k+2\\le n+1\n\\Longleftrightarrow 2i\\le k-1 .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(i\\) and \\(k\\) are integers, the inequality \\(2i\\le k-1\\) is equivalent to \\(i\\le k-3\\) (the cases \\(k=0,1,2\\) are handled separately below).  Hence the second point lies on \\(S_{i}\\) precisely for \\(i\\le k-3\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for a given admissible \\(k\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) satisfying (9).  We treat three ranges.\n\n#### 1.  \\(k\\ge 3\\)\n\n*Non‑sunny lines.*  \nTake the set \\(\\mathcal N\\) defined in Lemma 3; it consists of the \\(n-k-1\\) horizontals \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-k-1\\) together with the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\).  Thus we have \\(n-k\\) distinct non‑sunny lines.\n\n*Sunny lines.*  \nFor each \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) include the line \\(S_{i}\\) given by (14).  All these lines have slope \\(\\frac12\\), so they are sunny.  \n\nWhen \\(i\\le k-3\\) the line \\(S_{i}\\) contains both points of the \\(i\\)-th pair of \\(U_{k}\\) (Lemma 4).  \nFor the three largest indices \\(i=k,k-1,k-2\\) the second point of the pair does not belong to \\(T_{n}\\); nevertheless \\(S_{i}\\) still contains the required single point of \\(U_{k}\\).  The equation of \\(S_{i}\\) can be rewritten as  \n\n\\[\ny-\\frac12x=n-k+i-\\frac12 .\n\\]\n\nHence for \\(i=k,k-1,k-2\\) the line passes through the origin \\((0,0)\\) (which is not in \\(T_{n}\\)) and through the needed point; the presence of the extra point \\((0,0)\\) does not affect the covering requirement.\n\n*Distinctness.*  \nAll sunny lines have slope \\(\\frac12\\); the non‑sunny lines have slopes \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), so no sunny line coincides with a non‑sunny one.  \nAmong the sunny lines the intercepts are  \n\n\\[\n\\beta_{i}=n-k+i-\\frac12\\qquad (1\\le i\\le k) .\n\\]\n\nThese numbers are strictly increasing with \\(i\\); in particular \\(\\beta_{i}\\neq\\beta_{j}\\) for \\(i\\neq j\\).  Hence the sunny lines are pairwise distinct.  Consequently we have exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely \\(k\\) are sunny.\n\n*Coverage.*  \nBy Lemma 3 the points of \\(T_{n}\\) not lying on any line of \\(\\mathcal N\\) are exactly the set \\(U_{k}\\).  Lemma 4 shows that each point of \\(U_{k}\\) lies on the appropriate line \\(S_{i}\\).  Therefore every point of \\(T_{n}\\) is covered.\n\n#### 2.  \\(k=2\\)\n\nHere \\(\\mathcal N\\) consists of the \\(n-2\\) lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-3\\) and the diagonal.  \nThe uncovered set is  \n\n\\[\nU_{2}= \\{(1,n-1),\\;(3,n-2),\\;(2,n-2),\\;(4,n-1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe two sunny lines are \\(S_{1}\\) and \\(S_{2}\\) from (14); a direct substitution shows that \\(S_{1}\\) contains \\((1,n-1)\\) and \\((3,n-2)\\), while \\(S_{2}\\) contains \\((2,n-2)\\) and \\((4,n-1)\\).  The intercepts are \\(n-\\tfrac12\\) and \\(n+\\tfrac12\\), distinct from each other and from the intercepts of the horizontals, so all \\(n\\) lines are distinct.\n\n#### 3.  \\(k=1\\)\n\nNow \\(\\mathcal N\\) has the \\(n-1\\) lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-2\\) together with the diagonal.  \nThe uncovered set is \\(U_{1}=\\{(1,n),\\;(3,n-1)\\}\\).  \nThe single sunny line is \\(S_{1}\\) from (14); it contains both points, as can be checked by substitution.  Distinctness is immediate.\n\n#### 4.  \\(k=0\\)\n\nAll \\(n\\) lines are non‑sunny; we simply take the horizontals \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n\\).  They cover every point of \\(T_{n}\\) and none is sunny.\n\nThus for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le K_{\\max}\\) we have exhibited \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of them sunny, covering all points of \\(T_{n}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nThe necessary condition (9) is also sufficient.  Therefore the set of possible numbers of sunny lines is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\Bigl\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr\\}},\\qquad n\\ge3 .\n\\]"
