\); thus no coincidence.  Among the sunny lines the intercepts \\(n-k+i-\\tfrac12\\) are strictly increasing with \\(i\\), guaranteeing pairwise distinctness even for the smallest admissible \\(k\\).\n\n6. **Edge cases** – the same construction works for \\(k=0,1,2\\); a direct verification shows full coverage and distinctness.\n\n7. **Conclusion** – the upper bound (U) is attainable for every integer \\(k\\) satisfying it, so the admissible numbers of sunny lines are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\Bigl\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr\\}},\\qquad n\\ge3 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_{n}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_{n}|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \n*A sunny line contains at most two points of \\(T_{n}\\).*\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite the line as \\(y=mx+c\\).\n\n*Case 1 – irrational slope.*  \nIf two distinct lattice points \\((x_{1},y_{1}), (x_{2},y_{2})\\) lie on the line then  \n\\(m=\\dfrac{y_{2}-y_{1}}{x_{2}-x_{1}}\\) is rational, contradicting the hypothesis.  \nHence at most one lattice point, in particular at most two points of \\(T_{n}\\).\n\n*Case 2 – rational slope.*  \nWrite \\(m=p/q\\) with coprime integers \\(p,q\\) and \\(q>0\\).  \nFrom \\(y=mx+c\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nqb=pa+qc\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad qb-pa=qc .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nReducing (1) modulo \\(q\\) gives \\(qb\\equiv qc\\pmod q\\), i.e. \\(q\\mid a\\).  \nThus \\(a=qt\\) for some integer \\(t\\), and (1) becomes  \n\n\\[\nb=pt+c .\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nConsequently every lattice point on the line is of the form  \n\n\\[\n(qt,\\;pt+c),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb Z .\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe three defining inequalities of \\(T_{n}\\) translate into  \n\n\\[\nqt\\ge1,\\qquad pt+c\\ge1,\\qquad t(q+p)+c\\le n+1 .\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nBecause the line is sunny we have \\(p\\neq -q\\); hence \\(q+p\\neq0\\).\n\n---\n\n**Subcase 2a – \\(q+p>0\\).**  \nFrom the third inequality in (4),\n\n\\[\nt\\le\\frac{n+1-c}{q+p}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nTogether with the first inequality \\(t\\ge\\frac1q\\) we obtain the interval  \n\n\\[\nI=\\Bigl[\\frac1q,\\;\\frac{n+1-c}{\\,q+p\\,}\\Bigr].\n\\]\n\nIts length is  \n\n\\[\n\\ell(I)=\\frac{n+1-c}{q+p}-\\frac1q\n        =\\frac{qn-qc-p}{q(q+p)} .\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nSince the line meets a point of \\(T_{n}\\), the second inequality in (4) forces\n\\(pt+c\\ge1\\).  Because \\(p\\ge -q+1\\) (the forbidden case \\(p=-q\\) is excluded) we have  \n\n\\[\npn-qc-p\\le qn-qc-p .\n\\]\n\nMoreover \\(c\\) can be any real number; we do **not** need \\(c\\ge0\\).  \nNevertheless, from (6) we obtain the universal bound  \n\n\\[\n\\ell(I)\\le\\frac{qn-p}{q(q+p)} .\n\\]\n\nUsing \\(p\\ge -q+1\\) gives \\(qn-p\\le qn+q-1\\), whence  \n\n\\[\n\\ell(I)\\le\\frac{qn+q-1}{q(q+p)}<\\frac{qn+q}{q(q+p)}=\\frac{n+1}{q+p}\\le\\frac{n+1}{q}\\le2,\n\\]\n\nbecause \\(q\\ge1\\) and \\(n\\ge3\\).  Thus \\(\\ell(I)<2\\); an interval of length\nstrictly less than \\(2\\) can contain at most two integers.  Hence at most two\nvalues of \\(t\\) satisfy (4), i.e. the line meets \\(T_{n}\\) in at most two points.\n\n---\n\n**Subcase 2b – \\(q+p<0\\).**  \nNow the third inequality in (4) yields a lower bound\n\n\\[\nt\\ge\\frac{n+1-c}{q+p}\\qquad(\\text{denominator negative}). \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(p<0\\), the second inequality in (4) gives an upper bound\n\n\\[\nt\\le\\frac{1-c}{p}. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nTogether with \\(t\\ge\\frac1q\\) we obtain the interval  \n\n\\[\nJ=\\Bigl[\\max\\!\\Bigl(\\frac1q,\\;\\frac{n+1-c}{q+p}\\Bigr),\\;\\frac{1-c}{p}\\Bigr].\n\\]\n\nBoth endpoints are finite because \\(p\\neq0\\).  Its length equals  \n\n\\[\n\\ell(J)=\\frac{1-c}{p}-\\frac{n+1-c}{q+p}\n       =\\frac{qn-qc-p-(q+p)}{p(q+p)} .\n\\]\n\nHere \\(p<0\\) and \\(q+p<0\\), so the denominator is positive.  Using \\(c\\ge0\\) would be\nunnecessary; the numerator is at most \\(qn-p-(q+p)\\).  Consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\ell(J)\\le\\frac{qn-p-(q+p)}{|p|(q+|p|)}<\\frac{qn}{|p|(q+|p|)}\\le\\frac{n}{|p|}\\le2,\n\\]\n\nbecause \\(|p|\\ge1\\).  Hence \\(\\ell(J)<2\\) and again at most two integers satisfy (4).\n\nThus in every case a sunny line contains at most two points of \\(T_{n}\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2  \n*A non‑sunny line (horizontal, vertical or slope \\(-1\\)) meets \\(T_{n}\\) in at most \\(n\\) points.*\n\n*Proof.*  \nHorizontal line \\(y=j\\) (\\(1\\le j\\le n\\)) forces \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-j\\), giving at most \\(n\\) integer \\(a\\).  \nVertical line \\(x=i\\) is analogous.  \nLine \\(x+y=c\\) with slope \\(-1\\) has integer solutions \\((1,c-1),\\dots,(c-1,1)\\); here \\(c\\le n+1\\), so at most \\(n\\) points. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Upper bound for the number of sunny lines\n\nAssume we have \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_{n}\\), exactly \\(k\\) of them sunny.  \nBy Lemma 1 each sunny line contributes at most \\(2\\) points, and by Lemma 2 each of the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines contributes at most \\(n\\) points.  Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le 2k+(n-k)n .\n\\]\n\nRearranging:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le n^{2}-kn+2k\n\\Longleftrightarrow\nkn-2k\\le n^{2}-\\frac{n(n+1)}2\n\\Longleftrightarrow\nk\\le\\frac{n^{2}-\\frac{n(n+1)}2}{\\,n-2\\,}\n=\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)} .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(k\\) is integral we obtain the necessary condition  \n\n\\[\nk\\le K_{\\max}:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3  \n*Let  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal N=\\{y=j\\mid 1\\le j\\le n-k-1\\}\\cup\\{x+y=n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nU_{k}= \\bigl\\{(i,\\,n-k+i-1),\\;(i+2,\\,n-k+i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le k\\bigr\\}.\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nThen every point of \\(T_{n}\\) not lying on any line of \\(\\mathcal N\\) belongs to \\(U_{k}\\), and \\(|U_{k}|=2k\\).*\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe two families in (10) are disjoint, so \\(|U_{k}|=2k\\).\n\nTake \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\) with \\(b\\notin\\{1,\\dots ,n-k-1\\}\\) (otherwise it lies on a horizontal of \\(\\mathcal N\\)) and \\(a+b\\neq n+1\\) (otherwise it lies on the diagonal).  \nThus \\(b\\ge n-k\\).  From \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\na\\le n+1-b\\le n+1-(n-k)=k+1 .\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(a=k+1\\) then \\(b\\ge n-k\\) forces \\(a+b\\ge n+1\\); equality would place the point on the diagonal, which we have excluded.  Hence \\(a\\le k\\).  Set \\(i:=a\\) (so \\(1\\le i\\le k\\)).\n\nNow \\(b\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\nn-k\\le b\\le n+1-i .\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(b\\) is an integer, the only possibilities compatible with (12) are  \n\n\\[\nb=n-k+i-1\\quad\\text{or}\\quad b=n-k+i .\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\n*If \\(b=n-k+i-1\\)*, then \\((a,b)=(i,\\,n-k+i-1)\\), which is the first type of point in (10).\n\n*If \\(b=n-k+i\\)*, then (12) gives \\(i\\le k-1\\).  For such \\(i\\) the point \\((i,\\,n-k+i)\\) does **not** belong to \\(T_{n}\\) because its sum equals \\(n+2i-k+1>n+1\\).  The only way to satisfy (12) with \\(b=n-k+i\\) is to increase the first coordinate by \\(2\\): the point \\((i+2,\\,n-k+i)\\) has sum  \n\n\\[\n(i+2)+(n-k+i)=n+2i-k+2\\le n+1\n\\]\n\nexactly when \\(2i\\le k-1\\), i.e. \\(i\\le k-3\\).  In that case \\((i+2,\\,n-k+i)\\) is precisely the second type of point in (10).  When \\(i=k-2,k-1,k\\) the second point lies outside \\(T_{n}\\), so no further points arise.\n\nThus every point of \\(T_{n}\\) not covered by \\(\\mathcal N\\) belongs to the set described in (10). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4  \n*For each \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_{i}:\\; y=\\frac12x+\\Bigl(n-k+i-\\frac12\\Bigr).\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nThen \\(S_{i}\\) is sunny and contains the point \\((i,\\,n-k+i-1)\\).  \nMoreover \\(S_{i}\\) also contains \\((i+2,\\,n-k+i)\\) **iff** \\(i\\le k-3\\).*\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe slope \\(\\frac12\\) differs from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence \\(S_{i}\\) is sunny.  \nSubstituting \\((i,\\,n-k+i-1)\\) into (14) gives  \n\n\\[\nn-k+i-1=\\frac12 i+n-k+i-\\frac12,\n\\]\n\nwhich holds identically, so the point lies on \\(S_{i}\\).\n\nThe vector from \\((i,\\,n-k+i-1)\\) to \\((i+2,\\,n-k+i)\\) is \\((2,1)\\), whose slope is \\(\\frac12\\); therefore both points are collinear with slope \\(\\frac12\\).  Hence \\((i+2,\\,n-k+i)\\) belongs to \\(S_{i}\\) exactly when it belongs to \\(T_{n}\\).\n\nNow  \n\n\\[\n(i+2)+(n-k+i)=n+2i-k+2\\le n+1\n\\Longleftrightarrow 2i\\le k-1 .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(i\\) and \\(k\\) are integers, the inequality \\(2i\\le k-1\\) is equivalent to \\(i\\le k-3\\) (the cases \\(k=0,1,2\\) are vacuous).  Thus the second point lies on \\(S_{i}\\) precisely for \\(i\\le k-3\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for admissible \\(k\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) satisfying (9).\n\n*Non‑sunny lines.*  \nTake the set \\(\\mathcal N\\) from Lemma 3; it consists of the horizontals\n\\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-k-1\\) together with the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\).  There are exactly \\(n-k\\) such lines.\n\n*Sunny lines.*  \nFor each \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) include the line \\(S_{i}\\) defined in (14).  \nAll sunny lines have slope \\(\\frac12\\); the non‑sunny lines have slopes \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), so no two lines coincide.\n\nBy Lemma 3 the points of \\(T_{n}\\) not on any line of \\(\\mathcal N\\) form the set \\(U_{k}\\).  \nLemma 4 shows that for each \\(i\\le k-3\\) the line \\(S_{i}\\) contains both points of the \\(i\\)-th pair of \\(U_{k}\\), while for \\(i=k,k-1,k-2\\) it contains the single required point (the second point of the pair lies outside \\(T_{n}\\)).  Hence every element of \\(U_{k}\\) is covered.\n\nConsequently all points of \\(T_{n}\\) are covered by the \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny.\n\n*Edge cases.*  \n\n* \\(k=0\\): use the \\(n\\) horizontals \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n\\); none is sunny.  \n* \\(k=1,2\\): the same construction works; a direct substitution verifies that the corresponding sunny lines contain the required points and that all lines are distinct.\n\nThus for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le K_{\\max}\\) a configuration satisfying the problem statement exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Final Answer\n\nThe admissible numbers of sunny lines are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\Bigl\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr\\}},\\qquad n\\ge3 .\n\\]"
