le the non\u2011sunny lines have slopes \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); thus no coincidence.  Among the sunny lines the intercepts \\(n-k+i-\\tfrac12\\) are strictly increasing with \\(i\\), guaranteeing pairwise distinctness even for the smallest admissible \\(k\\).\n\n6. **Edge cases** \u2013 the same construction works for \\(k=0,1,2\\); a direct verification shows full coverage and distinctness.\n\n7. **Conclusion** \u2013 the upper bound (U) is attainable for every integer \\(k\\) satisfying it, so the admissible numbers of sunny lines are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\Bigl\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr\\}},\\qquad n\\ge3 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_{n}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_{n}|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f1  \n*A sunny line contains at most two points of \\(T_{n}\\).*\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite the line as \\(y=mx+c\\).\n\n*Case\u202f1 \u2013 irrational slope.*  \nIf two distinct lattice points \\((x_{1},y_{1}), (x_{2},y_{2})\\) lie on the line then  \n\\(m=\\dfrac{y_{2}-y_{1}}{x_{2}-x_{1}}\\) is rational, contradicting the hypothesis.  \nHence at most one lattice point, in particular at most two points of \\(T_{n}\\).\n\n*Case\u202f2 \u2013 rational slope.*  \nWrite \\(m=p/q\\) with coprime integers \\(p,q\\) and \\(q>0\\).  \nFrom \\(y=mx+c\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nqb=pa+qc\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad qb-pa=qc .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nReducing (1) modulo \\(q\\) gives \\(qb\\equiv qc\\pmod q\\), i.e. \\(q\\mid a\\).  \nThus \\(a=qt\\) for some integer \\(t\\), and (1) becomes  \n\n\\[\nb=pt+c .\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nConsequently every lattice point on the line is of the form  \n\n\\[\n(qt,\\;pt+c),\\qquad t\\in\\math