lar at most two points of \\(T_n\\)” is trivial; nevertheless the author does not explicitly note that a line with irrational slope can intersect \\(T_n\\) in at most one lattice point, which suffices for the lemma. The gap is minor but present because the reasoning is not fully spelled out.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Lemma 1 – Proof, rational slope, set‑up  \n\n> “Write \\(m=p/q\\) with coprime integers \\(p,q\\) and \\(q>0\\). … From \\(y=mx+c\\) we obtain \\(qb=pa+qc\\) … Reducing (1) modulo \\(q\\) gives … \\(q\\mid a\\). Thus \\(a=qt\\) … (3) \\((qt, pt+c)\\).”\n\n*Correct.* The algebraic manipulation is standard and valid.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Lemma 1 – Translation of inequalities (4)\n\n> “The three defining inequalities of \\(T_{n}\\) translate into …”\n\n*Correct.* Substituting \\(a=qt, b=pt+c\\) yields the three inequalities shown.\n\n---\n\n### 6. Lemma 1 – Subcase 2a (\\(q+p>0\\))\n\n#### 6.1 Derivation of interval \\(I\\) and its length (5)–(6)\n\n> “From the third inequality … \\(t\\le\\frac{n+1-c}{q+p}\\). … Together with the first inequality \\(t\\ge\\frac1q\\) we obtain the interval … Its length is … \\(\\ell(I)=\\frac{qn-qc-p}{q(q+p)}\\).”\n\n*Correct derivation of the interval.* The endpoints follow directly from (4).\n\n#### 6.2 Bounding \\(\\ell(I)\\)\n\n> “Since the line meets a point of \\(T_{n}\\), the second inequality … Because \\(p\\ge -q+1\\) … we obtain the universal bound \\(\\ell(I)\\le\\frac{qn-p}{q(q+p)}\\). Using \\(p\\ge -q+1\\) gives … \\(\\ell(I)<2\\).”\n\n**Justification Gap.**  \n- The claim “\\(p\\ge -q+1\\)” follows from the sunny condition \\(p\\neq -q\\), but the inequality \\(p\\ge -q+1\\) is not justified; \\(p\\) could be much larger (positive) or much smaller (negative) than \\(-q+1\\).  \n- The step “we obtain the universal bound \\(\\ell(I)\\le\\frac{qn-p}{q(q+p)}\\)” drops the term \\(-qc\\) without explaining why \\(-qc\\le0\\) (which would require \\(c\\ge0\\), not established).  \n- The subsequent chain of inequalities mixes se