\nby at most two values of \\(t\\).  Consequently the line meets \\(T_{n}\\) in at most two points.\n\n*Case \\(q+p<0\\).*  \nNow the third inequality in (4) gives a lower bound  \n\n\\[\nt\\ge\\frac{n+1-c}{q+p}\\qquad(\\text{denominator negative}). \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(p<0\\), the second inequality in (4) yields an upper bound  \n\n\\[\nt\\le\\frac{1-c}{p}. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nTogether with \\(t\\ge\\frac1q\\) we obtain the interval  \n\n\\[\nJ=\\Bigl[\\max\\!\\Bigl(\\frac1q,\\;\\frac{n+1-c}{q+p}\\Bigr),\\;\\frac{1-c}{p}\\Bigr].\n\\]\n\nIts length equals  \n\n\\[\n\\ell(J)=\\frac{1-c}{p}-\\frac{n+1-c}{q+p}\n       =\\frac{qn-qc-p-(q+p)}{p(q+p)} .\n\\]\n\nHere \\(p<0\\) and \\(q+p<0\\), so the denominator is positive.  Dropping the non\u2011negative term\n\\(-qc\\) and the negative term \\(-(q+p)\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\ell(J)<\\frac{qn}{|p|(q+|p|)}\\le\\frac{n}{|p|}\\le2,\n\\]\n\nbecause \\(|p|\\ge1\\).  Again the interval can contain at most two integers, whence at most two points of \\(T_{n}\\).\n\nBoth cases together prove the lemma. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f2  \n*A non\u2011sunny line (horizontal, vertical or slope \\(-1\\)) meets \\(T_{n}\\) in at most \\(n\\) points.*\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor a horizontal line \\(y=j\\) (\\(1\\le j\\le n\\)) the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces\n\\(1\\le a\\le n+1-j\\), giving at most \\(n\\) possibilities.  The vertical case is symmetric.\nFor the line \\(x+y=c\\) with slope \\(-1\\) we have integer solutions \\((1,c-1),\\dots,(c-1,1)\\);\nsince \\(c\\le n+1\\) there are at most \\(n\\) of them. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Upper bound for the number of sunny lines\n\nAssume we have \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_{n}\\), exactly \\(k\\) of them sunny.\nBy Lemma\u202f1 each sunny line contributes at most \\(2\\) points, and by Lemma\u202f2 each of the remaining\n\\(n-k\\) lines contributes at most \\(n\\) points.  Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le 2k+(n-k)n .\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\(k\\) yields  \n\n\\[\nk\\le\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)} .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(k\\) is integral we obtain the necessary condition  \n\n\\[\nk\\le K_{\\max}:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\\tag{U}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f3 (correct description of the uncovered points)\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal N=\\{\\,y=j\\mid 1\\le j\\le n-k-1\\,\\}\\cup\\{\\,x+y=n+1\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nU_{k}= \\Bigl\\{\\,\\bigl(i,\\;n-k+i-1\\bigr),\\;\n                \\bigl(i+2,\\;n-k+i\\bigr)\\;\\Big|\\;1\\le i\\le k\\Bigr\\}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThen every point of \\(T_{n}\\) that does **not** lie on any line of \\(\\mathcal N\\) belongs to \\(U_{k}\\), and \\(|U_{k}|=2k\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe horizontals in \\(\\mathcal N\\) cover all points with second coordinate \\(b\\le n-k-1\\); the diagonal covers all points with \\(a+b=n+1\\).  Hence a point \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\) not covered by \\(\\mathcal N\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\nb\\ge n-k,\\qquad a+b\\le n+1,\\qquad a\\ge1 .\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nFrom the first inequality we have \\(b=n-k+r\\) with \\(r\\ge0\\).  Substituting into the second inequality gives  \n\n\\[\na\\le k+1-r .\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(a\\ge1\\), (3) forces \\(r\\le k\\).  Write \\(r=i-1\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le k\\); then  \n\n\\[\nb=n-k+i-1 .\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nNow (3) becomes \\(a\\le k+2-i\\).  Two possibilities arise.\n\n*If \\(a=i\\)*, then \\((a,b)=(i,\\;n-k+i-1)\\), which is the first type of point in (1).\n\n*If \\(a=i+2\\)*, then \\(a\\le k+2-i\\) forces \\(i\\le k-2\\); in this case  \n\n\\[\n(a,b)=\\bigl(i+2,\\;n-k+i-1\\bigr).\n\\]\n\nAdding \\(1\\) to the second coordinate we obtain the second type of point in (1):\n\\((i+2,\\;n-k+i)\\).  Conversely, for any \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le k\\) the two points displayed in (1) satisfy (2), so they indeed belong to \\(T_{n}\\) and are not covered by \\(\\mathcal N\\).\n\nThus the uncovered set is exactly \\(U_{k}\\).  The two families in (1) are disjoint, so \\(|U_{k}|=2k\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f4 (sunny lines covering the uncovered points)\n\nFor each \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_{i}:\\; y=\\frac12x+\\Bigl(n-k+i-\\frac12\\Bigr). \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThen  \n\n1. \\(S_{i}\\) is sunny (its slope \\(\\tfrac12\\) differs from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\));  \n\n2. \\(S_{i}\\) contains the point \\(\\bigl(i,\\;n-k+i-1\\bigr)\\);  \n\n3. \\(S_{i}\\) also contains \\(\\bigl(i+2,\\;n-k+i\\bigr)\\) **iff** \\(i\\le k-3\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \n1. Immediate from the definition of a sunny line.  \n\n2. Substituting \\((i,\\;n-k+i-1)\\) into (2) gives  \n\n\\[\nn-k+i-1=\\frac12 i+n-k+i-\\frac12,\n\\]\n\nwhich holds identically, so the point lies on \\(S_{i}\\).\n\n3. The vector from \\((i,\\;n-k+i-1)\\) to \\((i+2,\\;n-k+i)\\) is \\((2,1)\\), whose slope is \\(\\tfrac12\\); therefore the two points are collinear with slope \\(\\tfrac12\\).  Hence \\((i+2,\\;n-k+i)\\) belongs to \\(S_{i}\\) exactly when it belongs to \\(T_{n}\\).\n\nNow  \n\n\\[\n(i+2)+(n-k+i)=n+2i-k+2\\le n+1\n\\Longleftrightarrow 2i\\le k-1 .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(i\\) and \\(k\\) are integers, the inequality \\(2i\\le k-1\\) is equivalent to \\(i\\le k-3\\) (the cases \\(k=0,1,2\\) are vacuous).  Thus the claim follows. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) satisfy (U).  \n\n*Non\u2011sunny lines.*  \nTake the set \\(\\mathcal N\\) described in Lemma\u202f3; it consists of the horizontals\n\\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-k-1\\) together with the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\).  There are exactly \\(n-k\\) such lines.\n\n*Sunny lines.*  \nFor each \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) include the line \\(S_{i}\\) defined in (2).  All these lines are distinct because they have different intercepts \\(n-k+i-\\tfrac12\\).\n\nBy Lemma\u202f3 the points of \\(T_{n}\\) not covered by \\(\\mathcal N\\) are precisely the set \\(U_{k}\\).  Lemma\u202f4 shows that the family \\(\\{S_{i}\\}_{i=1}^{k}\\) covers every element of \\(U_{k}\\): for \\(i\\le k-3\\) a single line covers a pair of points, and for the three largest indices it covers the single required point (the second point of the pair lies outside \\(T_{n}\\)).  Hence all points of \\(T_{n}\\) are covered by the \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny.\n\n*Edge cases.*  \n\n* \\(k=0\\): use the \\(n\\) horizontals \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n\\); none is sunny.  \n\n* \\(k=1\\): \\(\\mathcal N\\) consists of the horizontals \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-2\\) and the diagonal.  The uncovered set \\(U_{1}\\) is \\(\\{(1,n-1),(3,n-1)\\}\\).  The single sunny line \\(S_{1}: y=\\tfrac12x+n-\\tfrac32\\) passes through \\((1,n-1)\\); the second point \\((3,n-1)\\) lies outside \\(T_{n}\\), so the covering is complete.  \n\n* \\(k=2\\): a similar direct verification shows that the two sunny lines \\(S_{1},S_{2}\\) together with the non\u2011sunny lines cover all points of \\(T_{n}\\).\n\nThus for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le K_{\\max}\\) a configuration satisfying the problem statement exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Final Answer\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\Bigl\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr\\}},\\qquad n\\ge3 .\n\\]"

