nts with \\(b\\ge1\\)).  \n   Therefore\n   \\[\n   \\ell(I)\\le\\frac{qn-p}{q(q+p)}\\le\\frac{qn}{q(q+1)}\\le\\frac{n}{q+1}\\le2,\n   \\]\n   because \\(q\\ge1\\) and \\(n\\ge3\\).  \n   An interval of length \\(<2\\) can contain at most two integers, so at most two values of \\(t\\) satisfy the three inequalities, i.e. the line meets \\(T_n\\) in at most two points.\n\n   In the case \\(q+p<0\\) the three inequalities give a (possibly empty) interval\n   \\[\n   J=\\Bigl[\\max\\!\\Bigl(\\frac1q,\\;\\frac{n+1-c}{q+p}\\Bigr),\\;\\frac{1-c}{p}\\Bigr],\n   \\]\n   whose length equals\n   \\[\n   \\ell(J)=\\frac{1-c}{p}-\\frac{n+1-c}{q+p}\n          =\\frac{qn-qc-p-(q+p)}{p(q+p)} .\n   \\]\n   Here \\(p<0\\) and \\(q+p<0\\), so the denominator is positive.  Dropping the non‑negative term \\(-qc\\) and the negative term \\(-(q+p)\\) yields\n   \\[\n   \\ell(J)<\\frac{qn}{|p|(q+|p|)}\\le\\frac{n}{|p|}\\le2,\n   \\]\n   because \\(|p|\\ge1\\).  Again at most two integer values of \\(t\\) are possible.\n\n   Hence **any sunny line contains at most two points of \\(T_n\\).**\n\n3. **Lemma 2 (non‑sunny line ≤ \\(n\\) points).**  \n   Horizontal, vertical and slope \\(-1\\) lines intersect the triangle \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) in at most \\(n\\) lattice points; the verification is immediate.\n\n4. **Upper bound for the number of sunny lines.**  \n   Let a configuration contain \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines.  \n   By Lemma 1 each sunny line contributes ≤ 2 points, by Lemma 2 each non‑sunny line contributes ≤ \\(n\\) points.  Therefore\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le 2k+(n-k)n .\n   \\]\n   Rearranging,\n   \\[\n   2k\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}2-n^2+kn\n   \\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\n   2k\\ge kn-\\frac{n(n-1)}2\n   \\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\n   k\\le\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)} .\n   \\]\n   Since \\(k\\) is an integer we obtain the necessary condition\n   \\[\n   k\\le K_{\\max}:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\\tag{U}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Construction for every admiss