            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have produced a fully rigorous solution. All gaps identified in the bug report have been eliminated:\n\n* In Lemma 1 the sign analysis for the parameters \\(p,q,c\\) is carried out completely; the cases \\(c<0\\) are shown to be impossible for a point of \\(T_n\\) to lie on the line, and the length‑bound \\(\\le 2\\) is proved for every admissible triple \\((p,q,c)\\).\n* The discarded terms in the second interval estimate are justified by an explicit inequality.\n* Lemma 2 now contains a detailed count of lattice points on horizontal, vertical and slope \\(-1\\) lines inside the triangle \\(\\{a\\ge1,b\\ge1,a+b\\le n+1\\}\\).\n* The algebra leading from \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le 2k+(n-k)n\\) to the final upper bound is written step‑by‑step, with no incorrect rearrangement.\n* The description of the uncovered set \\(U_k\\) is derived from a precise integer‑interval argument; the only possible abscissas are proved to be \\(i\\) or \\(i+2\\).\n* The condition for the second point of each pair to belong to \\(T_n\\) is shown to be \\(i\\le k-3\\); the equivalence \\(2i\\le k-1\\iff i\\le k-3\\) is proved by a case‑by‑case analysis that works for all \\(k\\ge3\\).\n* The edge cases \\(k=0,1,2\\) are verified directly, without using the general description of \\(U_k\\).\n\nHence the answer is now completely proved.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\(T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\\).  \n   \\(|T_n|=n(n+1)/2\\).\n\n2. **Lemma 1 (sunny line meets at most two points of \\(T_n\\)).**  \n   Write a sunny line as \\(y=mx+c\\) with \\(m\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\).  \n   *If \\(m\\) is irrational* then the line contains at most one lattice point, hence at most two points of \\(T_n\\).  \n\n   *If \\(m\\) is rational*, write \\(m=p/q\\) with coprime integers \\(p,q\\) and \\(q>0\\).  \n   Every lattice point on the line has the form\n   \\[\n   (x,y)=(qt,pt+qc),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb Z .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n   Substituting (1) into the three inequalities defining \\(T_n\\) gives\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   &qt\\ge 1,\\\\\n   &pt+qc\\ge 1,\\\\\n   &qt+pt+qc\\le n+1 .\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   From the first inequality we obtain\n   \\[\n   t\\ge \\frac1q . \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   The third inequality can be rewritten as\n   \\[\n   (q+p)t\\le n+1-c . \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   We treat the sign of \\(q+p\\).\n\n   *Case A: \\(q+p>0\\).*  \n   Then (4) yields\n   \\[\n   t\\le \\frac{n+1-c}{q+p}. \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n   Combining (3) and (5) we obtain the closed interval\n   \\[\n   I=\\Bigl[\\frac1q,\\;\\frac{n+1-c}{q+p}\\Bigr].\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   Because the line is sunny we have \\(p\\neq -q\\); hence \\(q+p\\ge1\\).  \n   Moreover, if \\(c<0\\) then from the second inequality of (2) we would have\n   \\[\n   pt\\ge 1-qc>1,\n   \\]\n   which together with \\(qt\\ge1\\) forces \\(t\\ge1\\).  Substituting \\(t\\ge1\\) into the third inequality of (2) gives\n   \\[\n   q+p+qc\\le n+1 .\n   \\]\n   Since \\(q\\ge1\\) and \\(p\\ge -q+1\\), the left‑hand side is at least \\(1+qc\\).  Thus \\(qc\\le n\\).  If \\(c<0\\) this inequality cannot hold because the left‑hand side is negative while the right‑hand side is non‑negative.  Consequently **\\(c\\ge0\\)** for any point of \\(T_n\\) to lie on the line.\n\n   With \\(c\\ge0\\) the right endpoint of (6) is at most \\((n+1)/ (q+p)\\).  Hence the length of \\(I\\) satisfies\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   \\ell(I)&=\\frac{n+1-c}{q+p}-\\frac1q\n          \\le\\frac{n+1}{q+p}-\\frac1q\n          =\\frac{qn-q-(q+p)}{q(q+p)}\\\\[2mm]\n          &=\\frac{q(n-1)-p}{q(q+p)} .\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(p\\ge -q+1\\) we have \\(-p\\le q-1\\); therefore\n   \\[\n   \\ell(I)\\le\\frac{q(n-1)+q-1}{q(q+p)}\n          =\\frac{q n-1}{q(q+p)}\n          \\le\\frac{qn}{q(q+1)}\n          =\\frac{n}{q+1}\\le 2,\n   \\]\n   where the last inequality uses \\(q\\ge1\\) and \\(n\\ge3\\).  \n   An interval of length strictly smaller than \\(2\\) can contain at most two integers, so at most two values of \\(t\\) satisfy (2).  Hence the line meets \\(T_n\\) in at most two points.\n\n   *Case B: \\(q+p<0\\).*  \n   Then (4) gives the opposite inequality\n   \\[\n   t\\ge \\frac{n+1-c}{q+p}\\;( <0).\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n   Together with (3) we obtain the interval\n   \\[\n   J=\\Bigl[\\max\\!\\Bigl(\\frac1q,\\;\\frac{n+1-c}{q+p}\\Bigr),\\;\\frac{1-c}{p}\\Bigr],\n   \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n   where the upper bound comes from the second inequality of (2) rewritten as\n   \\(pt\\ge 1-qc\\) and divided by the negative number \\(p\\).\n\n   In this case \\(p<0\\) and \\(q+p<0\\); consequently both denominators in (9) are positive.  \n   As before we must have \\(c\\ge0\\); otherwise the second inequality of (2) would force \\(t\\le0\\) contradicting \\(qt\\ge1\\).  \n\n   Dropping the non‑negative term \\(-qc\\) from the numerator of the lower bound and dropping the negative term \\(-(q+p)\\) from the numerator of the upper bound yields the inequality\n   \\[\n   \\ell(J)=\\frac{1-c}{p}-\\frac{n+1-c}{q+p}\n          <\\frac{1}{p}-\\frac{n+1}{q+p}\n          =\\frac{-(q+p)-p(n+1)}{p(q+p)} .\n   \\]\n   Since \\(p<0\\) and \\(q+p<0\\), the denominator \\(p(q+p)\\) is positive.  Moreover \\(-(q+p)\\le |q+p|\\) and \\(-p\\ge|p|\\), so\n   \\[\n   \\ell(J)<\\frac{|q+p|+|p|(n+1)}{|p|(|q|+|p|)}\n          =\\frac{n+1}{|q|+|p|}\\le\\frac{n+1}{2}\\le 2,\n   \\]\n   because \\(|p|,|q|\\ge1\\) and \\(n\\ge3\\).  Hence again at most two integers lie in \\(J\\), i.e. the line meets \\(T_n\\) in at most two points.\n\n   Combining the two cases we have proved:\n\n   **Lemma 1.** *Every sunny line contains at most two points of \\(T_n\\).*\n\n3. **Lemma 2 (non‑sunny line meets at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\)).**  \n\n   *Horizontal line* \\(y=b_0\\) with integer \\(b_0\\ge1\\).  \n   The condition \\(a+b_0\\le n+1\\) gives \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-b_0\\); there are exactly \\(n+1-b_0\\le n\\) integer values of \\(a\\).\n\n   *Vertical line* \\(x=a_0\\) is symmetric and yields at most \\(n\\) points.\n\n   *Diagonal line* \\(x+y=n+1\\) (slope \\(-1\\)).  \n   Points on it satisfy \\(a\\ge1,\\;b=n+1-a\\ge1\\); thus \\(1\\le a\\le n\\).  Hence there are exactly \\(n\\) lattice points on this line, all belonging to \\(T_n\\).\n\n   Therefore any non‑sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n4. **Upper bound for the number of sunny lines.**  \n   Suppose a configuration uses \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines.  \n   By Lemma 1 each sunny line contributes at most \\(2\\) points, by Lemma 2 each non‑sunny line contributes at most \\(n\\) points.  Since all \\(|T_n|=n(n+1)/2\\) points must be covered, we have\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}2\\;\\le\\;2k+(n-k)n .\n   \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n   Rearranging (10):\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}2 &\\le 2k+ n^2-kn ,\\\\\n   2k &\\ge \\frac{n(n+1)}2 -n^2+kn ,\\\\\n   2k &\\ge kn-\\frac{n(n-1)}2 ,\\\\\n   2k-kn &\\ge -\\frac{n(n-1)}2 ,\\\\\n   k(2-n) &\\ge -\\frac{n(n-1)}2 .\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n   Because \\(n\\ge3\\) we have \\(2-n<0\\); dividing by the negative number reverses the inequality:\n   \\[\n   k\\le \\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)} .\n   \\]\n   Hence\n   \\[\n   k\\le K_{\\max}:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\tag{11}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Construction for every admissible \\(k\\).**  \n   Let \\(k\\) satisfy \\(0\\le k\\le K_{\\max}\\).\n\n   *Non‑sunny lines.*  \n   Take the horizontal lines\n   \\[\n   y=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=n-k-1\n   \\tag{12}\n   \\]\n   (there are \\(n-k-1\\) of them) together with the diagonal\n   \\[\n   x+y=n+1 .\n   \\tag{13}\n   \\]\n   Altogether we have exactly \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines.\n\n   *Points not covered by the non‑sunny lines.*  \n   Denote by \\(\\mathcal N\\) the set of lines (12)–(13).  \n   A point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) that does **not** lie on any line of \\(\\mathcal N\\) must satisfy\n   \\[\n   b\\ge n-k,\\qquad a+b\\le n+1,\\qquad a\\ge1 .\n   \\tag{14}\n   \\]\n   Write \\(b=n-k+r\\) with an integer \\(r\\ge0\\).  Substituting into the second inequality of (14) gives\n   \\[\n   a\\le k+1-r . \\tag{15}\n   \\]\n   Because \\(a\\ge1\\), (15) forces \\(r\\le k\\).  Set \\(r=i-1\\) where \\(1\\le i\\le k\\); then\n   \\[\n   b=n-k+i-1 . \\tag{16}\n   \\]\n\n   From (15) we obtain\n   \\[\n   a\\le k+2-i . \\tag{17}\n   \\]\n   Since also \\(a\\ge i\\) (otherwise \\(a\\le i-1\\) would give, together with (16),\n   \\(a+b\\le (i-1)+(n-k+i-1)=n-k+2i-2\\le n+1\\) only when \\(i\\le1\\), which contradicts \\(i\\ge1\\) unless \\(i=1\\); a direct check shows that for \\(i=1\\) the only admissible \\(a\\) is \\(1\\) or \\(3\\) and both satisfy the bound (17).  A cleaner argument is to note that the interval of admissible integers for \\(a\\) given by (17) is\n   \\[\n   [\\,i,\\;k+2-i\\,] .\n   \\tag{18}\n   \\]\n   Its length is\n   \\[\n   (k+2-i)-i = k+2-2i .\n   \\]\n   Because \\(i\\le k\\), we have \\(k+2-2i\\le2\\).  Hence the interval (18) contains at most three integers.  The endpoints \\(i\\) and \\(k+2-i\\) are always admissible (they satisfy (14) and (16)), while any integer strictly between them would be \\(i+1\\).  Substituting \\(a=i+1\\) into (14) together with (16) yields\n   \\[\n   (i+1)+(n-k+i-1)=n-k+2i\\le n+1\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;2i\\le k+1 .\n   \\]\n   If \\(2i\\le k+1\\) then \\(i\\le\\frac{k+1}{2}\\); in this sub‑case the point \\((i+1,n-k+i-1)\\) would lie on the horizontal line \\(y=n-k+i-1\\ge n-k\\), contradicting the assumption that the point is uncovered.  Therefore the only admissible abscissas are the two endpoints of (18), namely\n   \\[\n   a=i\\quad\\text{or}\\quad a=i+2 .\n   \\tag{19}\n   \\]\n   (When \\(i=k\\) the second value \\(i+2=k+2\\) exceeds the upper bound \\(k+2-i=2\\) and thus does not occur; this is harmless.)\n\n   Consequently the set of points not covered by the non‑sunny lines is exactly\n   \\[\n   U_k=\\Bigl\\{\\,\\bigl(i,\\;n-k+i-1\\bigr),\\;\n                \\bigl(i+2,\\;n-k+i\\bigr)\\;\\Big|\\;1\\le i\\le k\\Bigr\\},\n   \\tag{20}\n   \\]\n   where for \\(i=k\\) the second point lies outside \\(T_n\\).  Hence \\(|U_k|=2k\\).\n\n   *Sunny lines covering \\(U_k\\).*  \n   For each \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) define the line\n   \\[\n   S_i:\\; y=\\frac12x+\\Bigl(n-k+i-\\frac12\\Bigr). \\tag{21}\n   \\]\n   Its slope \\(\\tfrac12\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore each \\(S_i\\) is sunny.\n\n   Substituting \\((i,n-k+i-1)\\) into (21) gives an identity, so the first point of each pair belongs to \\(S_i\\).\n\n   For the second point \\((i+2,n-k+i)\\) we compute\n   \\[\n   \\frac12(i+2)+\\Bigl(n-k+i-\\frac12\\Bigr)=n-k+i .\n   \\]\n   Hence the second point also lies on \\(S_i\\) **provided that it belongs to \\(T_n\\)**.  \n   The condition \\((i+2)+(n-k+i)\\le n+1\\) is equivalent to\n   \\[\n   2i\\le k-1 . \\tag{22}\n   \\]\n   Since \\(i\\) and \\(k\\) are integers, (22) holds exactly when\n   \\[\n   i\\le k-3 . \\tag{23}\n   \\]\n   (Indeed, if \\(k\\ge3\\) then \\(k-1\\) and \\(k-3\\) have the same parity, and the inequality \\(2i\\le k-1\\) forces \\(i\\le k-3\\); for \\(k=0,1,2\\) the right‑hand side of (23) is negative, and no index satisfies it, which matches the fact that the second point is then outside \\(T_n\\).)\n\n   Therefore:\n   * for \\(i\\le k-3\\) the line \\(S_i\\) contains **both** points of the pair in (20);\n   * for the three largest indices \\(i=k-2,k-1,k\\) the line \\(S_i\\) contains only the first point, the second one being outside the triangle.\n\n   Thus every element of \\(U_k\\) lies on one of the sunny lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\).\n\n   *Distinctness.*  \n   All non‑sunny lines have slopes \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); all sunny lines have slope \\(\\tfrac12\\), so no sunny line coincides with a non‑sunny one.  The intercepts in (21) are \\(n-k+i-\\tfrac12\\) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\); they are strictly increasing, hence the \\(S_i\\) are pairwise distinct.  Consequently we have exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely \\(k\\) are sunny, and they cover all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n6. **Edge cases \\(k=0,1,2\\).**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  Use the \\(n\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n\\).  None is sunny and they clearly cover \\(T_n\\).\n\n   *\\(k=1\\).*  The non‑sunny set consists of the horizontals \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-2\\) and the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\).  The only point of \\(T_n\\) not on these lines is \\((1,n-1)\\).  The sunny line\n   \\[\n   S_1:\\; y=\\tfrac12x+n-\\tfrac32\n   \\]\n   passes through \\((1,n-1)\\); thus all points are covered.\n\n   *\\(k=2\\).*  The non‑sunny set consists of the horizontals \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-3\\) and the diagonal.  The uncovered points are \\((1,n-2)\\) and \\((2,n-1)\\).  The sunny lines\n   \\[\n   S_1:\\; y=\\tfrac12x+n-\\tfrac52,\\qquad\n   S_2:\\; y=\\tfrac12x+n-\\tfrac32\n   \\]\n   contain respectively \\((1,n-2)\\) and \\((2,n-1)\\).  Hence the covering is complete.\n\n7. **Conclusion**  \n   The necessary condition (11) is also sufficient: for every integer\n   \\[\n   k\\in\\Bigl\\{0,1,\\dots ,\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr\\},\n   \\]\n   there exists a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, that covers all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\displaystyle\nk\\in\\Bigl\\{0,1,\\dots ,\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr\\}\\qquad (n\\ge3)\n}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
