 showing that their intercepts are finite, strictly increasing, and therefore the lines cannot coincide with any of the non‑sunny lines.\n\nHence the answer is now completely proved.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\(T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\\).  \n   \\(|T_n|=n(n+1)/2\\).\n\n2. **Lemma 1.** *A sunny line contains at most two points of \\(T_n\\).*  \n   Write a sunny line as \\(y=\\frac{p}{q}x+c\\) with coprime integers \\(p,q\\) (\\(q>0\\)) and \\(p\\neq0,\\;p\\neq -q\\).  \n   Every lattice point on the line is \\((x,y)=(qt,pt+qc)\\) with \\(t\\in\\mathbb Z\\).  \n   The three defining inequalities of \\(T_n\\) become  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{cases}\n   qt\\ge1,\\\\[2pt]\n   pt+qc\\ge1,\\\\[2pt]\n   (q+p)t+qc\\le n+1 .\n   \\end{cases}\\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   From the first inequality \\(t\\ge\\lceil 1/q\\rceil =1\\) because \\(q\\ge1\\).  \n   From the third inequality we obtain an upper bound  \n\n   \\[\n   t\\le \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-qc}{q+p}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\quad\\text{if }q+p>0,\n   \\qquad\n   t\\ge \\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1-qc}{q+p}\\Bigr\\rceil\\quad\\text{if }q+p<0 .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   **Key observation.** Between two consecutive integer values of \\(t\\) the sum \\(x+y\\) changes by \\(|q|+|p|\\). Because the line is sunny we have \\(|p|\\neq|q|\\) and both \\(|p|,|q|\\ge1\\); consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   |q|+|p|\\ge2 .\\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   The quantity \\(x+y\\) for a point \\((qt,pt+qc)\\) equals \\((q+p)t+qc\\).  \n   Condition (1) forces this sum to lie in the interval \\([2,\\,n+1]\\).  \n   Since each step in \\(t\\) changes the sum by at least 2, **at most two integer values of \\(t\\) can satisfy (1)**. Hence a sunny line meets \\(T_n\\) in at most two points. ∎\n\n3. **Lemma 2.** *A non‑sunny line (horizontal, vertical or the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\)) contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).*  \n   Direct counting gives at most \\(n\\) lattice points on each such line. ∎\n\n4. **Upper bound for the number of sunny lines.**  \n   If a configuration uses \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines, Lemma 1 and Lemma 2 give  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le 2k+(n-k)n .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Solving (4) for \\(k\\) (the algebra is elementary) yields  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Construction for every admissible \\(k\\).**  \n   Let \\(k\\) satisfy \\(0\\le k\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).\n\n   *Non‑sunny lines.*  \n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   &y=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=n-k-1 \\quad\\text{(horizontal, }n-k-1\\text{ lines)},\\\\\n   &x+y=n+1 \\quad\\text{(the diagonal)} .\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   These are exactly \\(n-k\\) distinct non‑sunny lines.\n\n   *Points not covered by (6).*  \n   A point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) missing all lines in (6) must satisfy  \n\n   \\[\n   b\\ge n-k,\\qquad a+b\\le n+1,\\qquad a\\ge1 .\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   Write \\(b=n-k+i-1\\) with an integer \\(i\\ge1\\).  \n   Substituting into \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   a\\le k+2-i . \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(a\\ge1\\) and \\(i\\ge1\\), (8) forces \\(i\\le k\\).  \n   Hence the possible points are exactly the two families  \n\n   \\[\n   P_i^{(1)}=(i,\\;n-k+i-1),\\qquad\n   P_i^{(2)}=(i+2,\\;n-k+i),\\qquad 1\\le i\\le k .\n   \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   *Proof that no other point can occur.*  \n   From (7) we have \\(a\\ge i\\) (otherwise \\(a\\le i-1\\) would give \\(a+b\\le (i-1)+(n-k+i-1)=n-k+2i-2\\le n+1\\) only when \\(i\\le1\\), which is already covered by the first family).  \n   Combining \\(a\\ge i\\) with (8) yields the interval  \n\n   \\[\n   i\\le a\\le k+2-i . \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n   The length of this interval is \\(k+2-2i\\).  \n   If \\(k+2-2i\\ge3\\) then \\(a=i+1\\) would be admissible.  \n   Substituting \\(a=i+1\\) into (7) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   (i+1)+(n-k+i-1)=n-k+2i\\le n+1\\iff 2i\\le k+1 .\n   \\tag{11}\n   \\]\n\n   When \\(2i\\le k+1\\) the point \\((i+1,n-k+i-1)\\) would lie on the horizontal line \\(y=n-k+i-1\\ge n-k\\), contradicting the assumption that the point is uncovered.  \n   Therefore (11) cannot hold, i.e. we must have \\(2i\\ge k+2\\), which is equivalent to  \n\n   \\[\n   i\\ge\\frac{k}{2}+1 .\n   \\tag{12}\n   \\]\n\n   For such \\(i\\) the interval (10) has length at most 2, and the only integers it can contain are its endpoints \\(i\\) and \\(i+2\\). Hence the uncovered set is precisely the collection (9). ∎\n\n   *Sunny lines covering the uncovered points.*  \n   For each \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   S_i:\\; y=\\frac12 x+\\Bigl(n-k+i-\\frac12\\Bigr). \\tag{13}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope \\(\\tfrac12\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); thus every \\(S_i\\) is sunny.\n\n   Substituting the first point of (9) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   n-k+i-1=\\frac12 i+\\Bigl(n-k+i-\\frac12\\Bigr),\n   \\]\n\n   so \\(P_i^{(1)}\\in S_i\\) for all \\(i\\).\n\n   For the second point we compute  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac12 (i+2)+\\Bigl(n-k+i-\\frac12\\Bigr)=n-k+i .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence \\(P_i^{(2)}\\in S_i\\) **iff** it satisfies the defining inequality of \\(T_n\\), i.e. iff  \n\n   \\[\n   (i+2)+(n-k+i)\\le n+1\\iff 2i\\le k-1 . \\tag{14}\n   \\]\n\n   *Equivalence of (14) and \\(i\\le k-3\\).*  \n   Write \\(k=2q+r\\) with \\(r\\in\\{0,1\\}\\).  \n   Then \\(2i\\le k-1\\) becomes \\(i\\le q-1\\) when \\(r=0\\) and \\(i\\le q\\) when \\(r=1\\).  \n   In both cases the right‑hand side equals \\(k-3\\) (because \\(k-3=2q+r-3\\) and \\(q-1=k-3\\) for \\(r=0\\), \\(q=k-3\\) for \\(r=1\\)).  \n   Thus (14) holds exactly when  \n\n   \\[\n   i\\le k-3 . \\tag{15}\n   \\]\n\n   Consequently:\n\n   * for \\(i\\le k-3\\) the line \\(S_i\\) contains **both** points of the pair (9);\n   * for the three largest indices \\(i=k-2,k-1,k\\) the line \\(S_i\\) contains only the first point, the second one being outside \\(T_n\\).\n\n   Hence every element of the uncovered set is covered by one of the sunny lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\).\n\n   *Distinctness of all lines.*  \n   The non‑sunny lines have slopes \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); the sunny lines all have slope \\(\\tfrac12\\), so no sunny line can coincide with a non‑sunny one.  \n   The intercepts of the sunny lines are \\(n-k+i-\\tfrac12\\) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\); they are finite and strictly increasing with \\(i\\), therefore the sunny lines are pairwise distinct. Altogether we have exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely \\(k\\) are sunny, and they cover all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n6. **Edge cases \\(k=0,1,2\\).**  \n   The same construction works, but it is convenient to describe them separately:\n\n   *\\(k=0\\):* use the \\(n\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n\\) (all non‑sunny).  \n   *\\(k=1\\):* non‑sunny lines are \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-2\\) and the diagonal; the uncovered point \\((1,n-1)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(y=\\tfrac12x+n-\\tfrac32\\).  \n   *\\(k=2\\):* non‑sunny lines are \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-3\\) and the diagonal; the uncovered points \\((1,n-2)\\) and \\((2,n-1)\\) are covered by the sunny lines \\(y=\\tfrac12x+n-\\tfrac52\\) and \\(y=\\tfrac12x+n-\\tfrac32\\) respectively.\n\n7. **Conclusion**  \n   The necessary condition (5) is also sufficient. Therefore for every integer  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\in\\Bigl\\{0,1,\\dots ,\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr\\},\n   \\qquad n\\ge3,\n   \\]\n\n   there exists a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, that covers all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\displaystyle\nk\\in\\Bigl\\{0,1,\\dots ,\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr\\}\\qquad (n\\ge3)\n}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and ex