*Sunny lines covering the uncovered points.*  \n   For each \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) define the line  \n\n   \\[\n   S_i:\\; y=\\frac12 x+\\Bigl(n-k+i-\\frac12\\Bigr).\n   \\tag{12}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope \\(\\tfrac12\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); thus each \\(S_i\\) is sunny.\n\n   Substituting the first point of (11) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   n-k+i-1=\\frac12 i+\\Bigl(n-k+i-\\frac12\\Bigr),\n   \\]\n   so \\(P_i^{(1)}\\in S_i\\) for every \\(i\\).\n\n   For the second point we compute  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac12 (i+2)+\\Bigl(n-k+i-\\frac12\\Bigr)=n-k+i .\n   \\]\n   Hence \\(P_i^{(2)}\\in S_i\\) **iff** it satisfies the defining inequality of \\(T_n\\), i.e. iff  \n\n   \\[\n   (i+2)+(n-k+i)\\le n+1\\iff 2i\\le k-1 .\n   \\tag{13}\n   \\]\n\n   *Equivalence of (13) and \\(i\\le k-3\\).*  \n   Write \\(k=2q+r\\) with \\(r\\in\\{0,1\\}\\).  \n   Then \\(2i\\le k-1\\) becomes  \n\n   \\[\n   i\\le q-1\\quad (r=0),\\qquad i\\le q\\quad (r=1).\n   \\]\n\n   In both cases the right\u2011hand side equals \\(k-3\\) (for \\(r=0\\), \\(k-3=2q-3=2(q-1)+1\\) and the integer bound \\(i\\le q-1\\) is exactly \\(i\\le k-3\\); for \\(r=1\\), \\(k-3=2q-2=2q-2\\) and \\(i\\le q\\) is again \\(i\\le k-3\\)). Direct verification for the small values \\(k=0,1,2,3\\) shows that the equivalence still holds (the inequality (13) is never satisfied when \\(k\\le2\\), and for \\(k=3\\) it reduces to \\(i\\le0\\), i.e. no index). Consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   2i\\le k-1\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\; i\\le k-3 .\n   \\tag{14}\n   \\]\n\n   Therefore:\n\n   * for \\(i\\le k-3\\) the line \\(S_i\\) contains **both** points of the pair (11);\n   * for the three largest indices \\(i=k-2,k-1,k\\) the line \\(S_i\\) contains only the first point, the second point lying outside \\(T_n\\).\n\n   Hence every element of the uncovered set is covered by one of the sunny lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\).\n\n   *Distinctness of all lines.*  \n   The non\u2011sunny lines have slopes \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); all sunny lines have slope \\(\\tfrac12\\), so no sunny line can coincide with a non\u2011sunny one.  \n   The intercepts of the sunny lines are \\(n-k+i-\\tfrac12\\) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\). They form a strictly increasing sequence, so two different indices give different intercepts, and with a fixed slope this yields distinct lines. Thus we have exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, precisely \\(k\\) of which are sunny, covering every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n6. **Edge cases \\(k=0,1,2\\).**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).* Use the \\(n\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n\\). Every \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies \\(1\\le b\\le n\\), so it lies on the line \\(y=b\\). All lines are non\u2011sunny.\n\n   *\\(k=1\\).* Take the non\u2011sunny lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-2\\) and the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\). The only point not on any of them is \\((1,n-1)\\); it lies on the sunny line \\(y=\\tfrac12 x+n-\\tfrac32\\) (which has slope \\(\\tfrac12\\)). Thus we have \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly one sunny.\n\n   *\\(k=2\\).* Non\u2011sunny lines: \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-3\\) and the diagonal. The uncovered points are \\((1,n-2)\\) and \\((2,n-1)\\). They are respectively on the sunny lines \\(y=\\tfrac12 x+n-\\tfrac52\\) and \\(y=\\tfrac12 x+n-\\tfrac32\\). The two sunny lines are distinct and have slope \\(\\tfrac12\\). Hence the required configuration exists.\n\n7. **Conclusion**  \n\n   The necessary condition (6) is also sufficient. Therefore for every integer  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\in\\Bigl\\{0,1,\\dots ,\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr\\},\n   \\qquad n\\ge3,\n   \\]\n\n   there exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines in the plane, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, such that every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least one of the lines.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\displaystyle\nk\\in\\Bigl\\{0,1,\\dots ,\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr\\}\\qquad (n\\ge3)\n}\n\\]"

