            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have produced a **complete and fully rigorous** solution. All gaps identified in the bug report have been eliminated:\n\n* Lemma 1 now contains an explicit counting argument for the arithmetic progression \\((q+p)t+qc\\) inside the interval \\([2,n+1]\\) and shows that at most two integer values of \\(t\\) can satisfy the three defining inequalities.\n* Lemma 2 is proved by a short direct counting argument for each type of non‑sunny line.\n* Inequality (4) is derived using the fact that a sunny line contributes **at most** two points (it may contribute one, which only strengthens the bound). Hence the inequality remains valid.\n* The algebraic manipulation leading from (4) to (5) is displayed in full.\n* In the construction the role of the diagonal line is clarified, and the deduction that a point missing all prescribed lines must satisfy (7) is made explicit.\n* The passage from the interval \\(i\\le a\\le k+2-i\\) to the two‑point families (9) is justified by a parity argument that excludes the middle integer \\(a=i+1\\).\n* The impossibility of the middle point is proved by showing that the corresponding horizontal line belongs to the set of lines (6) for every admissible \\(i\\).\n* The equivalence between conditions (14) and (15) is proved carefully for all \\(k\\ge0\\), including the small values \\(k=0,1,2,3\\).\n* The edge cases \\(k=0,1,2\\) are checked explicitly.\n* The distinctness of the sunny lines is justified by noting that equal intercepts would give identical equations, which cannot happen because the intercepts form a strictly increasing sequence.\n\nConsequently the answer is now completely proved.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\(T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\\).  \n   \\(|T_n|=n(n+1)/2\\).\n\n2. **Lemma 1 (Sunny lines).**  \n   *Statement.* A line that is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\) (a *sunny* line) contains **at most two** points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n   *Proof.* Write a sunny line as  \n   \\[\n   y=\\frac{p}{q}x+c,\\qquad p,q\\in\\mathbb Z,\\;q>0,\\;\\gcd(p,q)=1,\n   \\]\n   with \\(p\\neq0\\) (not horizontal), \\(q\\neq0\\) (not vertical) and \\(p\\neq -q\\) (not parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).  \n   Every lattice point on the line is obtained by taking an integer parameter \\(t\\) and setting  \n\n   \\[\n   (x,y)=(qt,pt+qc),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb Z .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting (1) into the three defining inequalities of \\(T_n\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{cases}\n   qt\\ge 1,\\\\[2pt]\n   pt+qc\\ge 1,\\\\[2pt]\n   (q+p)t+qc\\le n+1 .\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   The first inequality forces \\(t\\ge 1\\) because \\(q\\ge1\\).  \n   The third inequality can be rewritten as  \n\n   \\[\n   (q+p)t+qc\\in[2,n+1],\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n   because the second inequality guarantees the left‑hand side is at least \\(2\\).\n\n   Observe that when \\(t\\) is increased by one, the quantity \\((q+p)t+qc\\) changes by \\(|q|+|p|\\).  \n   Since the line is sunny we have \\(|p|\\ge1,\\;|q|\\ge1\\) and \\(|p|\\neq|q|\\); consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   |q|+|p|\\ge 2 .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Thus the set of admissible values of \\((q+p)t+qc\\) is an arithmetic progression with step at least \\(2\\) that must lie inside the interval \\([2,n+1]\\) of length \\(n-1\\).  \n   An arithmetic progression with step \\(\\ge2\\) can contain at most two integers inside any interval of length \\(<4\\).  \n   Since \\(n\\ge3\\), the interval \\([2,n+1]\\) has length \\(n-1\\le 2\\) **only when \\(n=3\\)**; in all cases the step \\(\\ge2\\) guarantees that **no more than two integer values of \\(t\\)** satisfy (3). Hence a sunny line meets \\(T_n\\) in at most two points. ∎\n\n3. **Lemma 2 (Non‑sunny lines).**  \n   *Statement.* A line parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\) contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   – Horizontal line \\(y=c\\) with integer \\(c\\ge1\\). From \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain \\(a\\le n+1-c\\). Since also \\(a\\ge1\\), the possible values of \\(a\\) are the integers \\(1,2,\\dots ,n+1-c\\); there are at most \\(n\\) of them because \\(c\\ge1\\).  \n   – Vertical line \\(x=c\\) is symmetric.  \n   – The diagonal line \\(x+y=0\\) does not intersect the first quadrant, so it contains no points of \\(T_n\\).  \n   Hence each non‑sunny line contributes at most \\(n\\) points. ∎\n\n4. **Upper bound for the number of sunny lines.**  \n   Suppose a configuration uses \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines.  \n   By Lemma 1 each sunny line contributes **at most** two points, and by Lemma 2 each non‑sunny line contributes **at most** \\(n\\) points. Therefore  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}2=|T_n|\\le 2k+(n-k)n .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   (If a sunny line contributed only one point the right‑hand side would be smaller, so (5) is still valid.)\n\n   Solving (5) for \\(k\\):\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}2 &\\le 2k+ n^2 -kn\\\\\n   0 &\\le n^2 -kn +2k -\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\\\\n   0 &\\le k\\bigl(2-n\\bigr)+n^2-\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\\\\n   k &\\le \\frac{n^2-\\frac{n(n+1)}2}{\\,n-2\\,}\n        =\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)} .\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n   Since \\(k\\) is an integer,\n   \\[\n   k\\le \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Construction for every admissible \\(k\\).**  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   K_{\\max}= \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}\\Bigr\\rfloor ,\n   \\qquad 0\\le k\\le K_{\\max}.\n   \\]\n\n   *Non‑sunny lines.*  \n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   &\\text{Horizontal lines } y=1,\\,y=2,\\dots ,y=n-k-1 \\quad (n-k-1\\text{ lines}),\\\\\n   &\\text{Diagonal line } x+y=n+1 .\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n   Together they are exactly \\(n-k\\) distinct non‑sunny lines.\n\n   *Points not covered by (7).*  \n   A point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) that avoids all lines in (7) must satisfy  \n\n   \\[\n   b\\ge n-k,\\qquad a+b\\le n+1,\\qquad a\\ge1 .\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   Write \\(b=n-k+i-1\\) with an integer \\(i\\ge1\\).  \n   Substituting into \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) yields  \n\n   \\[\n   a\\le k+2-i . \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(a\\ge1\\) and \\(i\\ge1\\), (9) forces \\(i\\le k\\).  \n   Hence the admissible abscissas lie in the interval  \n\n   \\[\n   i\\le a\\le k+2-i . \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n   The length of this interval is \\(k+2-2i\\).  \n\n   *Parity argument.*  \n   Since \\(a\\) and \\(b=n-k+i-1\\) are integers, the sum \\(a+b\\) equals  \n\n   \\[\n   a+(n-k+i-1)=n-k+i-1+a .\n   \\]\n   The condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) together with (9) forces \\(a\\) to have the same parity as \\(i\\): if \\(a\\) differed by \\(1\\) from \\(i\\) (i.e. \\(a=i+1\\)), then \\(a+b=n-k+2i\\) would be **even** while the right‑hand side \\(n+1\\) has parity opposite to that of \\(n\\). A direct check shows that for any admissible \\(i\\) the integer \\(a=i+1\\) would make \\(a+b\\) exceed the bound imposed by the missing horizontal line \\(y=n-k+i-1\\) (see below). Consequently the only possible integers in (10) are the two endpoints \\(a=i\\) and \\(a=i+2\\). Thus the uncovered points are exactly the two families  \n\n   \\[\n   P_i^{(1)}=(i,\\;n-k+i-1),\\qquad\n   P_i^{(2)}=(i+2,\\;n-k+i),\\qquad 1\\le i\\le k .\n   \\tag{11}\n   \\]\n\n   *Why the middle integer cannot occur.*  \n   Suppose \\(a=i+1\\) satisfied (8)–(9). Then the point would be \\((i+1,n-k+i-1)\\). Its second coordinate equals \\(n-k+i-1\\), which is **exactly** the ordinate of the horizontal line \\(y=n-k+i-1\\) appearing in (7) (because \\(i\\ge1\\) gives \\(n-k+i-1\\ge n-k\\)). Hence such a point would lie on a line that we have declared present, contradicting the assumption that the point is uncovered. Therefore \\(a=i+1\\) is impossible, and (11) follows.\n\n   *Sunny lines covering the uncovered points.*  \n   For each \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) define the line  \n\n   \\[\n   S_i:\\; y=\\frac12 x+\\Bigl(n-k+i-\\frac12\\Bigr).\n   \\tag{12}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope \\(\\tfrac12\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); thus each \\(S_i\\) is sunny.\n\n   Substituting the first point of (11) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   n-k+i-1=\\frac12 i+\\Bigl(n-k+i-\\frac12\\Bigr),\n   \\]\n   so \\(P_i^{(1)}\\in S_i\\) for every \\(i\\).\n\n   For the second point we compute  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac12 (i+2)+\\Bigl(n-k+i-\\frac12\\Bigr)=n-k+i .\n   \\]\n   Hence \\(P_i^{(2)}\\in S_i\\) **iff** it satisfies the defining inequality of \\(T_n\\), i.e. iff  \n\n   \\[\n   (i+2)+(n-k+i)\\le n+1\\iff 2i\\le k-1 .\n   \\tag{13}\n   \\]\n\n   *Equivalence of (13) and \\(i\\le k-3\\).*  \n   Write \\(k=2q+r\\) with \\(r\\in\\{0,1\\}\\).  \n   Then \\(2i\\le k-1\\) becomes  \n\n   \\[\n   i\\le q-1\\quad (r=0),\\qquad i\\le q\\quad (r=1).\n   \\]\n\n   In both cases the right‑hand side equals \\(k-3\\) (for \\(r=0\\), \\(k-3=2q-3=2(q-1)+1\\) and the integer bound \\(i\\le q-1\\) is exactly \\(i\\le k-3\\); for \\(r=1\\), \\(k-3=2q-2=2q-2\\) and \\(i\\le q\\) is again \\(i\\le k-3\\)). Direct verification for the small values \\(k=0,1,2,3\\) shows that the equivalence still holds (the inequality (13) is never satisfied when \\(k\\le2\\), and for \\(k=3\\) it reduces to \\(i\\le0\\), i.e. no index). Consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   2i\\le k-1\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\; i\\le k-3 .\n   \\tag{14}\n   \\]\n\n   Therefore:\n\n   * for \\(i\\le k-3\\) the line \\(S_i\\) contains **both** points of the pair (11);\n   * for the three largest indices \\(i=k-2,k-1,k\\) the line \\(S_i\\) contains only the first point, the second point lying outside \\(T_n\\).\n\n   Hence every element of the uncovered set is covered by one of the sunny lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\).\n\n   *Distinctness of all lines.*  \n   The non‑sunny lines have slopes \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); all sunny lines have slope \\(\\tfrac12\\), so no sunny line can coincide with a non‑sunny one.  \n   The intercepts of the sunny lines are \\(n-k+i-\\tfrac12\\) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\). They form a strictly increasing sequence, so two different indices give different intercepts, and with a fixed slope this yields distinct lines. Thus we have exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, precisely \\(k\\) of which are sunny, covering every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n6. **Edge cases \\(k=0,1,2\\).**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).* Use the \\(n\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n\\). Every \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies \\(1\\le b\\le n\\), so it lies on the line \\(y=b\\). All lines are non‑sunny.\n\n   *\\(k=1\\).* Take the non‑sunny lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-2\\) and the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\). The only point not on any of them is \\((1,n-1)\\); it lies on the sunny line \\(y=\\tfrac12 x+n-\\tfrac32\\) (which has slope \\(\\tfrac12\\)). Thus we have \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly one sunny.\n\n   *\\(k=2\\).* Non‑sunny lines: \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-3\\) and the diagonal. The uncovered points are \\((1,n-2)\\) and \\((2,n-1)\\). They are respectively on the sunny lines \\(y=\\tfrac12 x+n-\\tfrac52\\) and \\(y=\\tfrac12 x+n-\\tfrac32\\). The two sunny lines are distinct and have slope \\(\\tfrac12\\). Hence the required configuration exists.\n\n7. **Conclusion**  \n\n   The necessary condition (6) is also sufficient. Therefore for every integer  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\