one of the later inequality (5). Because Lemma 1 is false, the bound derived for the number of sunny lines is unsupported.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Lemma 2 (Non‑sunny lines)\n\n> “A line parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\) contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).”\n\n*Correct reasoning for horizontal and vertical lines. The diagonal \\(x+y=0\\) indeed contains no points of \\(T_n\\). No error here.*\n\n---\n\n### 4. Upper bound for the number of sunny lines (inequality (5))\n\n> “Suppose a configuration uses \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines.  \n> By Lemma 1 each sunny line contributes **at most** two points, and by Lemma 2 each non‑sunny line contributes **at most** \\(n\\) points. Therefore  \n> \\[\n> \\frac{n(n+1)}2=|T_n|\\le 2k+(n-k)n .\n> \\tag{5}\n> \\]”\n\n*Because Lemma 1 is false, the bound “each sunny line contributes at most two points” is unjustified. Hence inequality (5) is not guaranteed. This is a **Critical Error** that propagates to the final answer.*\n\n---\n\n### 5. Solving inequality (5) to obtain (6)\n\n> The algebraic manipulation from (5) to (6) is correct **provided** (5) holds.\n\n*Since (5) is not established, the derived bound (6) is not a valid necessary condition.*\n\n---\n\n### 6. Construction for every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nThe construction assumes the bound (6) is correct and then attempts to exhibit a configuration for each \\(k\\) up to that bound.\n\n*Because the bound itself is unsupported, the claim “the necessary condition (6) is also sufficient” is unfounded. Moreover, the construction contains additional issues:*\n\n#### 6.1. Parity argument\n\n> “Since \\(a\\) and \\(b=n-k+i-1\\) are integers, the sum \\(a+b\\) equals … The condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) together with (9) forces \\(a\\) to have the same parity as \\(i\\): … A direct check shows that for any admissible \\(i\\) the integer \\(a=i+1\\) would make \\(a+b\\) exceed the bound … Consequently the only possible integers in