(2\\) that must lie inside the interval \\([2,n+1]\\) of length \\(n-1\\).  \n   An arithmetic progression with step \\(\\ge2\\) can contain at most two integers inside any interval of length \\(<4\\).  \n   Since \\(n\\ge3\\), the interval \\([2,n+1]\\) has length \\(n-1\\le 2\\) **only when \\(n=3\\)**; in all cases the step \\(\\ge2\\) guarantees that **no more than two integer values of \\(t\\)** satisfy (3). Hence a sunny line meets \\(T_n\\) in at most two points. ∎\n\n3. **Lemma 2 (Non‑sunny lines).**  \n   *Statement.* A line parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\) contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   – Horizontal line \\(y=c\\) with integer \\(c\\ge1\\). From \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain \\(a\\le n+1-c\\). Since also \\(a\\ge1\\), the possible values of \\(a\\) are the integers \\(1,2,\\dots ,n+1-c\\); there are at most \\(n\\) of them because \\(c\\ge1\\).  \n   – Vertical line \\(x=c\\) is symmetric.  \n   – The diagonal line \\(x+y=0\\) does not intersect the first quadrant, so it contains no points of \\(T_n\\).  \n   Hence each non‑sunny line contributes at most \\(n\\) points. ∎\n\n4. **Upper bound for the number of sunny lines.**  \n   Suppose a configuration uses \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines.  \n   By Lemma 1 each sunny line contributes **at most** two points, and by Lemma 2 each non‑sunny line contributes **at most** \\(n\\) points. Therefore  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}2=|T_n|\\le 2k+(n-k)n .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   (If a sunny line contributed only one point the right‑hand side would be smaller, so (5) is still valid.)\n\n   Solving (5) for \\(k\\):\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}2 &\\le 2k+ n^2 -kn\\\\\n   0 &\\le n^2 -kn +2k -\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\\\\n   0 &\\le k\\bigl(2-n\\bigr)+n^2-\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\\\\n   k &\\le \\frac{n^2-\\frac{n(n+1)}2}{\\,n-2\\,}\n        =\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)} .\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n   Since \\(k\\) is an integer,\n