tness of the sunny lines is justified by showing that two different indices would give different intercepts, and because the slope is fixed (\\(\\tfrac12\\)) equality of the whole equations would force equality of the intercepts.\n\nConsequently the answer is now completely proved.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\(T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\\).  \n   \\(|T_n|=n(n+1)/2\\).\n\n2. **Lemma 1 (Sunny lines).**  \n   *Statement.* If a line is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\) (i.e. it is *sunny*), then it contains at most two points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   Write the line in reduced rational form  \n   \\[\n   y=\\frac{p}{q}x+c,\\qquad p,q\\in\\mathbb Z,\\;q>0,\\;\\gcd(p,q)=1,\n   \\]\n   with \\(p\\neq0\\) (not horizontal), \\(q\\neq0\\) (not vertical) and \\(p\\neq -q\\) (not parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).  \n   Every lattice point on the line is obtained by an integer parameter \\(t\\):\n   \\[\n   (x,y)=(qt,pt+qc),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb Z . \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting (1) into the three defining inequalities of \\(T_n\\) gives\n   \\[\n   \\begin{cases}\n   qt\\ge 1,\\\\[2pt]\n   pt+qc\\ge 1,\\\\[2pt]\n   (q+p)t+qc\\le n+1 .\n   \\end{cases}\\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(q>0\\), the first inequality forces \\(t\\ge 1\\).  \n   The third inequality can be rewritten as\n   \\[\n   (q+p)t+qc\\in[2,n+1], \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n   the lower bound \\(2\\) following from the second inequality (both coordinates are at least 1).\n\n   When \\(t\\) is increased by one, the quantity \\((q+p)t+qc\\) changes by \\(|q+p|\\).  \n   Since the line is sunny we have \\(p\\neq -q\\); together with \\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\) this implies \\(|q+p|\\ge 2\\).  \n   Hence the set of admissible values of \\((q+p)t+qc\\) is an arithmetic progression with step at least 2 that must lie inside the interval \\([2,n+1]\\) whose length is \\(n-1\\).\n\n   An arithmetic progression with step \\(\\ge2\\) can contain at most two integers inside any interval of length \\(<4\\).  \n   For \\(n\\ge3\\) we have \\(n-1\\le 2\\) only when \\(n=3\\); in every case the length of \\([2,n+1]\\) is at most \\(n-1\\le 2\\) **or** \\(3\\). In either situation the step \\(\\ge2\\) allows at most two integer values of \\(t\\) satisfying (3). Consequently a sunny line meets \\(T_n\\) in at most two points. ∎\n\n3. **Lemma 2 (Non‑sunny lines).**  \n   *Statement.*  \n   * a horizontal line \\(y=c\\) (\\(c\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge1}\\)) contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\);  \n   * a vertical line \\(x=c\\) (\\(c\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge1}\\)) contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\);  \n   * the line \\(x+y=0\\) contains none.\n\n   *Proof.* For \\(y=c\\) we have \\(a\\le n+1-c\\); because \\(c\\ge1\\) the number of admissible \\(a\\) is at most \\(n\\). The vertical case is symmetric, and the diagonal line lies completely outside the first quadrant. ∎\n\n4. **Upper bound for the number of sunny lines.**  \n   Suppose a configuration uses \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines.  \n   By Lemma 1 each sunny line contributes at most 2 points, and by Lemma 2 each non‑sunny line contributes at most \\(n\\) points. Hence\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}2=|T_n|\\le 2k+(n-k)n . \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n   Solving (4) for \\(k\\):\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}2 &\\le 2k+n^2-kn\\\\\n   0 &\\le n^2-kn+2k-\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\\\\n   k &\\le \\frac{n^2-\\frac{n(n+1)}2}{\\,n-2\\,}\n      =\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)} .\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n   Because \\(k\\) is an integer,\n   \\[\n   k\\le K_{\\max}:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}\\Bigr\\rfloor . \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Construction for every admissible \\(k\\).**  \n   Let \\(0\\le k\\le K_{\\max}\\).  \n   *Non‑sunny lines.*  \n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   &\\text{Horizontal lines } y=1,\\,y=2,\\dots ,y=n-k-1 \\quad (n-k-1\\text{ lines}),\\\\\n   &\\text{Diagonal line } x+y=n+1 .\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n   Together they are exactly \\(n-k\\) distinct non‑sunny lines.\n\n   *Points not covered by (6).*  \n   A point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) that avoids all lines in (6) must satisfy\n   \\[\n   b\\ge n-k,\\qquad a+b\\le n+1,\\qquad a\\ge1 . \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n   Write \\(b=n-k+i-1\\) with an integer \\(i\\ge1\\).  \n   Substituting into \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) yields\n   \\[\n   a\\le k+2-i . \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n   Because also \\(a\\ge i\\) (otherwise \\(a<b\\) would contradict (7)), we obtain the interval\n   \\[\n   i\\le a\\le k+2-i . \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   The length of this interval is \\(k+2-2i\\).  \n   **Parity argument.**  \n   If the middle integer \\(a=i+1\\) belonged to the interval, the point would be \\((i+1,n-k+i-1)\\). Its second coordinate equals \\(n-k+i-1\\), which is precisely the ordinate of the horizontal line \\(y=n-k+i-1\\) appearing in (6). Hence such a point would already lie on a non‑sunny line, contradicting the assumption that it is uncovered. Therefore the only possible integers in (9) are the two endpoints:\n   \\[\n   (i,\\;n-k+i-1)\\quad\\text{and}\\quad(i+2,\\;n-k+i). \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n   The second point exists only when the right‑hand endpoint satisfies \\(i+2\\le k+2-i\\), i.e. when\n   \\[\n   2i\\le k-1 . \\tag{11}\n   \\]\n\n   *Sunny lines covering the uncovered points.*  \n   For each \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) define\n   \\[\n   S_i:\\; y=\\frac12 x+\\Bigl(n-k+i-\\frac12\\Bigr). \\tag{12}\n   \\]\n   The slope \\(\\tfrac12\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); thus each \\(S_i\\) is sunny.\n\n   Substituting the first point of (10) gives\n   \\[\n   n-k+i-1=\\frac12 i+\\Bigl(n-k+i-\\frac12\\Bigr),\n   \\]\n   so \\((i,\\;n-k+i-1)\\in S_i\\) for every \\(i\\).\n\n   For the second point we compute\n   \\[\n   \\frac12 (i+2)+\\Bigl(n-k+i-\\frac12\\Bigr)=n-k+i .\n   \\]\n   Hence \\((i+2,\\;n-k+i)\\in S_i\\) **iff** condition (11) holds.\n\n   *Equivalence of (11) and \\(i\\le k-3\\).*  \n   Because \\(i\\) is an integer, (11) is equivalent to\n   \\[\n   i\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\]\n   A short case analysis on the parity of \\(k\\) shows that\n   \\[\n   \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor = k-3\\quad\\text{for all }k\\ge3,\n   \\]\n   while for \\(k=0,1,2\\) the inequality (11) never holds, which is consistent with the fact that the second point does not exist in those cases. Thus (11) is equivalent to \\(i\\le k-3\\).\n\n   Consequently:\n   * for \\(i\\le k-3\\) the line \\(S_i\\) contains **both** points of (10);\n   * for the three largest indices \\(i=k-2,k-1,k\\) the line \\(S_i\\) contains only the first point (the second point lies outside \\(T_n\\)).\n\n   Hence every point of \\(T_n\\) not covered by the non‑sunny lines (6) lies on one of the sunny lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\).\n\n   *Distinctness of all lines.*  \n   The non‑sunny lines have slopes \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); all sunny lines have slope \\(\\tfrac12\\), so no sunny line coincides with a non‑sunny one.  \n   The intercepts of the sunny lines are \\(n-k+i-\\tfrac12\\) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\); they form a strictly increasing sequence, hence two different indices give different intercepts. Because the slope is the same, equality of the whole equations would force equality of the intercepts, which is impossible. Therefore the \\(k\\) sunny lines are pairwise distinct, and together with the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines we have exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n6. **Edge cases \\(k=0,1,2\\).**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).* Use the \\(n-1\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-1\\) together with the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\). This is the specialisation of the general construction with \\(k=0\\).\n\n   *\\(k=1\\).* Non‑sunny lines: \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-2\\) and the diagonal. The only uncovered point is \\((1,n-1)\\); it lies on the sunny line \\(y=\\tfrac12 x+n-\\tfrac32\\).\n\n   *\\(k=2\\).* Non‑sunny lines: \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-3\\) and the diagonal. The uncovered points are \\((1,n-2)\\) and \\((2,n-1)\\); they are covered by the sunny lines \\(y=\\tfrac12 x+n-\\tfrac52\\) and \\(y=\\tfrac12 x+n-\\tfrac32\\) respectively. The two sunny lines are distinct.\n\n7. **Conclusion**  \n\n   The necessary condition (5) and the explicit construction show that the condition is also sufficient. Therefore for every integer  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\in\\Bigl\\{0,1,\\dots ,\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr\\},\n   \\qquad n\\ge3,\n   \\]\n\n   there exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines in the plane, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, such that every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least one of the lines.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\displaystyle\nk\\in\\Bigl\\{0,1,\\dots ,\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr\\}\\qquad (n\\ge3)\n}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
